WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.310
- So welcome everybody.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.310
Let me open the public board
meeting of NHS England.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.490
First of all, we have some
apologies for absence today.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.800
Emily Lawson and Simon Wessely,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.530
Jeremy Townsend, Mark Walport,

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.300
Mary Watkins who was with us this morning

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.040
in the private board session,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.200
but can't be with us this afternoon,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.263
and also Tanuj Kapilashrami.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.500
Are there any new interests

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.550
any members of the board need to declare?

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.450
Thank you very much.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.460
Can I take the minutes
of the board meeting

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.470
held on the 25th of July?

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:41.303
Can I approve those?

00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:42.180
- [Person Off-Camera] Yes.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.280
- Thank you very much.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.963
Let me just very briefly provide

00:00:46.963 --> 00:00:50.010
a sort of a Chair's
update on recent activity.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.160
First of all, actually,
I should start by saying

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.100
that it's absolutely great
to be here in Blackpool

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.020
for our latest board session.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:03.020
We hold two or three boards
in the year outside London,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.982
and our last board was in Norwich.

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:06.900
And today, bless you, Amanda.
- Sorry.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.360
- Today we're in Blackpool

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.210
and we're absolutely delighted to be here.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.440
I'd like to thank our colleagues

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.637
from the North West Regional team

00:01:14.637 --> 00:01:18.210
and the Lancashire and South Cumbria ICB,

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.860
not only for helping
with the arrangements,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.960
but actually for hosting us.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.460
And we're very pleased to have

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:24.690
Kevin Lavery, Chief Executive,

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:27.750
and Dr. Andy Knox, Associate
Medical Director in the ICB

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:31.410
here with us and to discuss
actually later on in the agenda

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.480
integrated care.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.350
And actually just to share,

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.850
that was very much a focus of a visit

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.830
that you kindly arranged for me yesterday

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:41.013
when I went to the
Whitegate Health Centre.

00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:45.000
It was a brilliant visit, actually.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.740
I don't quite know how to describe it.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:50.097
Except, I think I said to
you it felt like it was a,

00:01:50.097 --> 00:01:53.330
it was like a sort of a
mini hospital for minors.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.270
And actually Amanda Doyle
here corrected me and said,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:01.830
it's actually more a
hospital without beds.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.560
And the reason for that
actually is I had no idea

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.150
that there was so much provision

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.180
of so many different
services in that location

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.013
that it was a

00:02:10.013 --> 00:02:11.550
one, it was a sizeable building,

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:15.150
three stories and of size,

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.733
modern, which was great to see,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.240
and the urgent treatment centre,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.600
sexual health care, dental services,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.940
community diagnostic centre

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.600
offering MRI and CT and other
diagnostic capabilities,

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.640
podiatry, children's mental health,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.750
adult mental health and other services.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.720
So I was, it was a brilliant thing to see

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.670
and it made me think
actually, if that's the old,

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:43.920
is that the polyclinic
model that was talked about

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.120
some years ago?

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.700
But congratulations to
those who conceived it

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.747
and built it, actually.

00:02:48.747 --> 00:02:50.853
And it was a brilliant visit.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.180
Now I know the board actually,

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:56.400
so what we tend to do when
we hold board meetings

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.840
outside London of course is actually

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:58.920
the day after the board,

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.060
the directors then split
up and go and visit

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.310
various parts of the system.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:08.548
And tomorrow we're doing that as well.

00:03:08.548 --> 00:03:10.050
And we're going,

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.640
I think there are five different visits

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.680
around different parts of the system.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.660
And that's a really
helpful sort of learning

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.580
for certainly the non-executives

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.980
and perhaps the executives as well.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.260
But also actually it's
an ability to communicate

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.690
and to hear directly from people

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:25.680
who are out on the front line

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.400
and here particularly in Lancashire,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.353
South Cumbria and in Blackpool.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.640
Let me turn to some housekeeping.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.863
I'd just like to start
to her embarrassment,

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.400
no doubt her embarrassment by thanking

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.280
Professor Dame Helen Stokes-Lampard,

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.280
who as we know will be leaving
us pretty imminently actually

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.120
to go to the other side of the world

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.990
to become Chief Medical Officer
for Health in New Zealand.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:59.280
And even though Helen joined
us for a relatively short time,

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.020
she's been absolutely pivotal,

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.340
and thank you for it,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:06.540
in our work on both primary
care and on our quality agenda.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.850
And of course you've also
been a force for good

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:12.000
within the NHS more
broadly for many years,

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.910
both as a GP for your community

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.810
as well as Chair of the Academy
of Medical Royal Colleges

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.840
during 2020 to 2023

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.150
through the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.250
And also in your previous role

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.860
as Chair of the Royal College
of General Practitioners

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.710
from 2016 to 2019.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:36.330
Helen, you are an amazing
person and an amazing medic

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:40.050
and we wish you huge
success in New Zealand

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:42.330
and the leadership you
showed in your role here

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:43.440
and more broadly in your career

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.790
I know you'll continue

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.860
and that's to New Zealand's great benefit.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.513
So many thanks.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:54.333
Just a series of engagements
if I might share with you.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.480
So I went on a very good visit

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.940
to St. Ann's and Chase Farm Hospital

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.030
as part of the North London
Mental Health Partnership.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.570
And it was very good to again
engage with frontline staff

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:11.630
to see in particular
actually how proud they were

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:16.630
of the accreditation that
St. Ann's had received.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.600
And it was also very, most interesting yet

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.720
to see how a crisis hub
actually worked and operated.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:22.620
It had been explained to me,

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:25.200
but to see it in action and
to see the pressures on it

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.740
and how those challenges and
pressures were dealt with

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.230
was really sort of informative and helpful

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:32.850
and how they're there for support people

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:34.080
in mental health crisis,

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.780
but also actually assisting police forces

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.670
reducing detention.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.290
And that's clearly something very relevant

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.540
to the paper we'll be
discussing later today

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.820
I think on community-based support

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:48.060
for people with serious mental illness.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.940
I've had a whole series
of different meetings,

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.350
so with colleagues obviously
from the department

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.900
and from other arm's length bodies

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.210
and also meetings with Confed

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.240
and with providers and with the assembly,

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.650
with the Health Foundation Chief Executive

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:07.043
with a group of chairs
from mental health trusts

00:06:07.043 --> 00:06:10.230
as well as actually the quarterly meeting

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.330
with all the ICB chairs.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:13.860
So we do three meetings in the day,

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:17.040
so 42 ICBs split into groups of 14

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.890
and that's a really
helpful, helpful engagement.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.620
And then we've had two
Genomics England boards

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:26.880
where I sit on the board for the NHS

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.560
and also on Monday we,

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:31.650
I chaired the Genomics
Medicine Services Board.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:35.730
Again, I raise that because it
was a very good announcement

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.320
this morning.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.903
And Steve Powis, where are you Steve?

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.080
Congratulations on a, yeah,

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.550
you were on the "Today" programme again,

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.020
you're quite often on
the "Today" programme,

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.550
but I thought you covered

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:50.190
what's a really very good announcement

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:51.510
of brilliantly.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:56.510
And it's the announcement
of the new generation

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:59.520
whole genome sequencing.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.337
And so now actually working
with Genomics England,

00:07:02.337 --> 00:07:06.150
the NHS I think through 13 or 14 hospitals

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:07.950
and going to grow to about 40

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:11.250
has now tested so far 500 babies,

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.740
so, newborns

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.470
and we are testing whole
genome and scanning

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:18.300
for 200 rare diseases.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.130
And actually, if one thinks
about the way genomics

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.500
will play in the future
model of healthcare,

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.717
this is a, again, a brilliant step forward

00:07:24.717 --> 00:07:27.780
and I think demonstrates
the continued innovation

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.333
that we see in the health service.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:36.480
On some of the work of the board,

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:40.290
I mean this morning we did
discuss in the private board

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:43.797
Lord Ara Darzi's very good report

00:07:43.797 --> 00:07:45.720
and clearly and as I know Amanda will say

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:46.920
and has said publicly,

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.320
it's a very challenging report,

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.020
but one which actually shows

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.573
the significant challenges
that the NHS is dealing with

00:07:54.573 --> 00:07:57.750
that many of us actually recognise.

00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:00.360
We also had two good sessions as a board

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.460
in the past weeks with Sally Warren

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:06.720
and her colleagues as she
brings together and starts

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.990
to construct the plan for
the 10-Year Health Plan.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.540
And we're also looking
forward to engaging with that

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:16.053
much more as that continues to evolve.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.060
We had a strategy board
about three weeks or so ago

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:24.870
and one, it was to that
board that Sally came,

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.660
that was for one of the
engagements we've had with her.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.060
But we also discussed the
health and work agenda

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.193
and that obviously lies,

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:35.430
ties in with the announcement

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:37.770
that the Secretary of State made last week

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.570
on focusing GIRFT support,

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:41.670
so Get It Right First Time support,

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:43.620
in areas with higher numbers of people

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:46.650
who are economically inactive.

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.330
But it's clearly an area
where there are clearly

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.690
many more opportunities
for the NHS to contribute

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:53.440
to the government's growth mission.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.820
And on top of that, we
had, as the board knows,

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:58.110
a training session,

00:08:58.110 --> 00:08:59.880
a very good training
session on patient safety

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:01.320
for senior leaders

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:03.180
and taking us through
what board colleagues

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:06.030
all across the NHS need to know

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:09.600
and look for so that they can
get on top of problems early

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:11.610
and hopefully head them off.

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:15.390
Now safety is something
we often talk about here.

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:17.580
We did again this morning in an update

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.790
led first by Robert Lechler,

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:22.680
thinking about the quality committee

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:23.910
and the quality agenda

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:26.190
and also Amanda looking
at how we put quality

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:30.330
at the pivot or the keel of NHS strategy.

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:33.210
So, and safety is something
we talk about often

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:34.560
and can take many forms.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:37.290
And actually Steve, I do
just want to pick up one,

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.720
which was the paediatric audiology story

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:42.480
that ran across the weekend,

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.990
last weekend's newspapers
as just one example.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:49.140
And I know you and Sue Hill
have been working on this

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:50.700
for some time and you
might want to give us

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:52.500
some perspective just on that.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:54.720
- Yes, happy to do that, Chair.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:55.770
So first and foremost,

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:58.320
important to say we understand
just how worrying it must be

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.780
for parents to learn about the challenges

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:03.300
within paediatric audiology services.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:05.880
And I know that for
those directly impacted,

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:08.100
this will be an incredibly
distressing time

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.310
and to them of course we
extend our sincere apologies.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.530
It's a very serious issue
and we've been undertaking,

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:14.363
as you've said,

00:10:14.363 --> 00:10:17.073
extensive work to
address it as a priority.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:20.460
The issues first came to light
because following concerns

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:23.130
identified in audiology
services in Scotland,

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:25.500
we undertook our own
review into hearing data

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.237
for every baby born in
England from 2018 to 2023.

00:10:30.237 --> 00:10:32.040
And this identified five services

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:34.680
where significantly fewer
babies had been diagnosed

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.290
with permanent childhood
hearing impairment

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.270
than we would've expected.

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:41.610
And then further analysis
of these service identified

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:43.230
a number of concerns that suggested

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.110
there could be more
widespread quality issues.

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:48.090
So in response, last year we established

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:51.090
a paediatric hearing services
improvement programme

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.700
under the leadership of
Dame Sue Hill, as you said,

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:56.070
the Chief Scientific Officer for England.

00:10:56.070 --> 00:10:57.780
And the programme board which supports Sue

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.000
and oversees this work includes providers,

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.920
includes NHS regions,

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:03.420
multidisciplinary experts,

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:04.590
professional bodies

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:06.960
and importantly, patient groups.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:09.900
And a year ago we wrote
to Integrated Care Boards

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.360
who are the commissioners
of these local services,

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:14.070
asking them to assess their services

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:16.710
against a set of national recommendations.

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:19.200
And where services did not
meet these recommendations,

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.650
we additionally asked ICBs
to take immediate action

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.933
to implement the necessary changes.

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:30.450
Since then, all 140 NHS
paediatric audiology services

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:32.670
in England have been assessed.

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:35.370
This work, which
concluded in early August,

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:37.890
has identified 90 services

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:39.120
where we are currently have,

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:41.460
we currently have insufficient assurance

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:44.250
of the quality of services being provided.

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:46.110
And these services will be now subject

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:48.750
to further in-depth expert reviews

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:51.420
including determining where
children should be called back

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:52.800
for reassessment.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:55.650
And we wrote to ICBs once again last week

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:58.983
with detailed guidance on this next stage.

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:02.580
Now centrally, NHS England
will be providing support

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:04.110
to local commissioners by providing

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:05.460
additional central funding

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:08.610
for senior scientists
with audiology expertise

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:12.000
to provide expert oversight
and input into the reviews.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.310
And this will also ensure
that tests for patients

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:17.670
are carried out as quickly
and safely as possible.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:19.380
And in parallel, during the last year

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:22.080
we've hailed national and
regional learning sessions

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.600
for clinical staff
focused on driving forward

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:26.940
major improvements in
professional standards

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:28.950
and testing techniques.

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:30.780
But we understand the
need to move at pace.

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:32.490
And so the national aim is to complete

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:35.520
all patient reassessments
that arise from this process

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:37.451
by March, 2025

00:12:37.451 --> 00:12:40.260
and to ensure that all
patients are either discharged

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:42.630
or placed on an appropriate
treatment pathway

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:45.227
by September, 2025.

00:12:45.227 --> 00:12:47.430
And we also understand this
is a challenging timeframe

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:49.920
for our ICBs and local services,

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:52.590
but we also recognise that
particularly for children,

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:55.426
hearing issues need to be identified early

00:12:55.426 --> 00:12:57.960
and we know that this wait
will feel unacceptably long

00:12:57.960 --> 00:12:58.793
for many.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:01.890
We also recognise
concerns about the impact

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:03.480
that this additional work may have

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.270
on those awaiting audiology services

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:10.440
and we are working hard
to minimise any impact.

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:12.960
And while the NHS continues
the review process,

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.680
we're also working to ensure
that this never happens again.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:18.180
Frankly, we need to future-proof

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.220
England's audiology services for patients

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:21.810
and for their families.

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:23.760
And this, for example, may include

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.620
potential reconfiguration of services

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:27.690
to include, to ensure
they're more resilient

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:29.250
and more sustainable,

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:31.650
a greater focus on
quality and accreditation

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:34.410
and greater standardisation of training.

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:36.120
All this work is complex,

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:37.860
changes will take time,

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.600
but we are committed
to driving improvements

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:40.560
in these services.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:42.600
And I'd like finally to thank all of those

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:44.460
who I know are working incredibly hard

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.470
at both national and local level

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.870
to address the challenges that we see.

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:51.436
- Steve, thank you for the update.

00:13:51.436 --> 00:13:54.810
I think it was important
to reflect on that

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:56.820
and not just 'cause it
was covered in the news,

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:00.240
but actually I hadn't realised
that was the situation.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:03.210
And thank you for the insight
to the work that's ongoing now

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:06.240
and the urgency with which
it will be be pursued.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:07.173
So, thank you.

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:11.500
I have referenced the 10-year plan

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:15.540
and again, we discussed it
somewhat in the private board

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:16.373
this morning

00:14:16.373 --> 00:14:19.320
and we're not the authors
of the 10-year plan,

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:21.480
but we clearly have a major contribution

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:23.930
to make to it as essentially
the delivery arm of,

00:14:25.363 --> 00:14:28.320
for health in the country.

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:33.180
And I, musing with Amanda, would hope

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:35.550
that a number of the
things we've talked about

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:37.020
over the past months,

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:39.630
which we would call our main priorities,

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:41.910
I would sometimes call them
the enabling priorities

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:44.340
of what would feature in the 10-year plan

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:45.173
as we look forward,

00:14:45.173 --> 00:14:47.100
including potentially the considerations

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:51.905
of how the offer of health service,

00:14:51.905 --> 00:14:54.930
the model of care may well change

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:57.177
as we think hard about
the impact of technology

00:14:57.177 --> 00:15:00.510
and changing skills as well
as changing societal demand,

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:02.400
but are there different models of care?

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.190
And I would hope that is
very much a feature of it.

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:07.080
Along also with,

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.870
if I thought about some of the areas

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:12.390
we've been very strongly engaged on here,

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:15.690
the long-term workforce
plan, really important.

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:17.790
We had a conversation
a couple of boards ago

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:20.250
about the Long-Term
Infrastructure Strategy,

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:22.660
which you could call the
Long-Term Capital Plan.

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:25.140
I mean, it's the equivalent of
the Long-Term Workforce Plan.

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:26.760
And clearly actually one of the things

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:29.220
that Ara, Lord Darzi pointed out was

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:30.540
capital under investment.

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:33.060
And we've clearly done a lot of work.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:35.580
It's a very strong forensic foundation,

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:38.520
an audit effectively of the
current state of our state

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.620
and our technology and our diagnostics

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:42.220
and clearly has

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:45.930
good information and direction in it.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:47.583
We've talked about capacity,

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:50.160
both primary but also secondary,

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:51.930
and whether we should
think about capacity.

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:54.750
What I, in a way I
would call thematically.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.250
So you can't just think capacity,

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:57.360
how do I build more?

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:59.880
But you can think about
how is the current capacity

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:02.670
we've got being best at being utilised

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:06.060
and can we therefore tilt
demand, shape demand,

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:09.510
so we utilise that capacity
in a different way?

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:12.150
And that leads to things like the pilot

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:13.890
that is ongoing at the moment,

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:16.878
a very good pilot on frailty.

00:16:16.878 --> 00:16:19.440
Frailty tends to be
linked to older people,

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:22.110
but frailty and also the very good pilot,

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:25.830
which I hope will be a
feature in the 10-year plan

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:28.317
around primary care where again,

00:16:28.317 --> 00:16:31.413
NHS England is doing a very
significant piece of work.

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:34.260
The other area is just to reference

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:38.940
I would hope that the continued rollout

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:42.750
and devolution to the ICBs continues

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:45.750
even though we do know that
current performance is variable

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:47.820
across a range of measures.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:50.370
But actually the idea of
devolving close to the community

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:53.010
and then working across the boundary

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:56.490
into local authorities, social care

00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:58.680
and into the community is a very, I think,

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:01.290
helpful direction of the
board wholly supports.

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:03.630
So, continuing to support that

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:07.710
and to help shape it to be
more successful would feature.

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:08.730
And then my last two,

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:10.050
which I think are really important

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:12.090
and Ara Darzi captured one with the,

00:17:12.090 --> 00:17:14.040
I think it was called
the tilt to technology

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:15.000
or some such phrase,

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.240
but a very good reference just
on how powerful technology

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:19.650
is going to be

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:21.450
and actually where do we,

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:22.740
how do we think about that

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.990
in terms of properly architecting it

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:26.700
and then what choices we make

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:28.860
in no doubt a constrained environment

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:30.150
about which technologies,

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:32.820
at which area of the system

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:34.560
do we prioritise for investment.

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:37.470
But the role of technology
in changing health provision

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:41.100
must surely be a very strong
feature of the 10-year plan.

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:43.410
And then lastly, for
me, it's just innovation

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:45.390
and referencing that
announcement this morning

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:47.400
about the new generation,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:51.690
newborn whole genome
sequencing announcement,

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:55.080
that innovation, or the
cancer vaccine launchpad

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:57.300
or the bespoke or tailored vaccinations

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:59.700
I think for lung cancer and bowel cancer,

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:02.490
or just actually the way
we used genomics today,

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:03.840
going back to that example.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:08.100
So 700,000 patients tested last year

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:12.030
for whole genome or panel or
single test for 200 cancers,

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:13.890
7,000 rare diseases.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:16.290
What a remarkable world.

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:19.320
And actually that, the forward innovation

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:20.820
that I think we can see,

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:25.650
I think must be features
of the 10-year health plan.

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:27.970
Now I just want to round off

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:32.100
on the topic of finance.

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:35.220
Julian is joining us hopefully imminently.

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:38.250
He's being caught up in another

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:41.130
and really quite important
obviously discussion

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:45.000
and he'll cover the financial position.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.910
Look, suffice it to say as we know,

00:18:47.910 --> 00:18:50.290
the picture is very challenging

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:54.000
and it's very challenging
across the service

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:56.070
and across the systems.

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:58.500
And it's not for the want of effort

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:00.900
on the part of people here,

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:04.710
the board, the executives, NHS England,

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:07.740
or colleagues out throughout
the systems in the NHS

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:10.800
that we do face a very difficult

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:13.320
and challenging financial picture.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:14.950
Now the fact that

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:19.170
spend on agency staff
is at its lowest ever,

00:19:19.170 --> 00:19:20.730
so its lowest on record

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:24.150
again is a testament to
the focus and work on that.

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:27.570
And that's at the same time
as activity continues to grow

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:29.640
and remains at record levels

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:30.663
in many areas.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:34.350
And that record level of health provision

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:35.220
that we're seeing today,

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:38.010
today the NHS provides
record levels of healthcare,

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:39.990
absolute record levels of healthcare.

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:43.770
It's a testament to how hard
people across the system

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:46.770
are working and to make taxpayers' money

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:48.813
go as far as humanly possible.

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:51.090
But what I want to say,

00:19:51.090 --> 00:19:53.530
I've read a lot of commentary this week

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:56.850
from the system

00:19:56.850 --> 00:20:00.210
on the financial position
and how difficult it is

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:01.860
and I hear that,

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:05.220
we hear that and we understand it.

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:08.850
But I would add that there
is no one here at the board

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:10.230
or indeed at NHS England

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:13.920
that doesn't recognise the
reality of that challenge

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:16.170
and that we've been
consistently transparent

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:20.220
about the very significant
financial challenges we face.

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:22.140
But I would also add
that the government has

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:26.250
the very difficult job
of setting the funding

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:27.877
and mandate for the NHS.

00:20:28.835 --> 00:20:33.090
And it is our role as
senior leaders in the NHS,

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:35.250
whether that's the NHS England board

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:38.430
or senior leaders in the wider system

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:42.150
to fully take on the responsibility

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:45.090
for making this work

00:20:45.090 --> 00:20:49.170
as best as possible within
the financial envelope

00:20:49.170 --> 00:20:50.760
that we have been given.

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:52.320
So I was reading some comments, actually.

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:53.820
It's a very challenging situation.

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:55.350
We all own that challenge,

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:56.280
we all own that challenge

00:20:56.280 --> 00:21:00.210
and it's really important
that we face to it.

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:01.710
So those, that's what I wanted to say

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:06.030
by way of introductory remarks

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:08.670
and at which point can
I hand to you, Amanda,

00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:09.930
Chief Executive's update,

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:11.820
and then hopefully
Julian will be in in time

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:14.940
for the CFO update, but.

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:17.160
- Yeah Richard, thank
you very much indeed.

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:20.070
And look, can I start by
just echoing Richard's thanks

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:21.750
to colleagues from the region

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:24.270
and from Lancashire and South Cumbria ICB

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:25.350
for hosting us

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:28.500
and to Kevin and Andy for
joining our meeting today.

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:30.570
As Richard says, I think we're
very much looking forward

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:31.980
to having dinner tonight

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:33.090
with a wider group of colleagues

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:34.410
and then we've got some great visits

00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:36.750
to look forward to tomorrow.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:40.140
Also like to add just to
further her embarrassment

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:43.560
my personal thanks to
Helen, as Richard said,

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:45.690
you know, your time with us
Helen has been a lot shorter

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:47.610
than any of us would have wanted,

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:51.030
but our loss is very
much New Zealand's gain

00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:53.460
and Helen, your contribution to the NHS,

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:54.360
to the medical profession,

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:55.590
to primary care in particular

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:58.170
means you'll be leaving I
think with the best wishes

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.993
of the whole of the NHS,
not just of this board.

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:05.100
Just while we're talking about people,

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:06.690
I know you are aware of this,

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:09.750
but we are absolutely
delighted to have appointed

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:11.910
Louise Shepherd who is the Chief Executive

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:14.280
of Alder Hey Children's Hospital

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:17.070
to the role of Regional
Director for the North West,

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:18.900
so the region that we're in at the moment.

00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:22.440
And I would just like to
thank again Michael Gregory,

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:24.810
who's done a great job
in covering that role

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:27.333
in the interim since
Richard Barker retired.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:33.750
So since our last meeting,
an awful lot has happened

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:37.230
and I just wanted to pass,

00:22:37.230 --> 00:22:39.300
well I didn't want to let this,

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:40.590
let us have this conversation

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:42.090
without just noting that of course

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:44.970
one of the things that the
North West has had to deal with

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:48.300
since we were last together
were the horrific events

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:52.170
in Southport at the end of July.

00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:56.100
And I would just like to
thank all of my colleagues

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:58.560
in the NHS who contributed to the care

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:00.780
of those children and adults

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:02.610
killed and injured that day.

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:05.340
And that is as ever a testament

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:10.340
to the way that the NHS comes
together around communities

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:15.030
and around patients at the
most challenging of times.

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:16.950
This won't do justice
to all of the colleagues

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:19.350
who are involved, but just to name a few,

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:21.150
that was North West Ambulance Service,

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:23.760
that was Southport and Formby
District General Hospital,

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:26.670
Ormskirk Hospital, Alder
Hey Children's Hospital,

00:23:26.670 --> 00:23:28.590
Aintree University Hospital,

00:23:28.590 --> 00:23:30.480
Manchester Children's Hospital,

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:33.810
and then of course colleagues
from across the ICS

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:35.640
and from the region.

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:39.060
I mean, so many people did
an incredible job that day

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:40.500
and over the days that followed,

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:43.320
whether that was supporting
the victims and their families

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:47.820
or dealing with the mindless
riots that followed.

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:50.520
Their professionalism and
that of the police, fire

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:52.560
and other services deserves our respect.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:54.330
But we also know,

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:56.010
and we'll talk about this I think perhaps

00:23:56.010 --> 00:23:58.830
when we'll talk about mental
health services a bit later on,

00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:00.330
but for some of those individuals,

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:04.140
those events really will
take a long-term toll.

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:07.110
So they deserve our ongoing support

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:09.453
and we have committed to that.

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:13.320
Since we last met, we've
also continued to work

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:14.400
with the new government.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:17.280
We've seen a really
welcome focus on engagement

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:18.450
between the Secretary of State

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:20.100
and the wider NHS

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:21.720
including regional visits

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:26.720
and a national meeting
with Trust and ICB CEOs.

00:24:27.030 --> 00:24:28.380
And as Richard says,

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:30.210
we've obviously been working
with the new government

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:31.710
on early policy initiatives.

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:35.220
Most notably that included the
Further Faster 20 initiative

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:36.750
which the Secretary of State announced

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:39.570
at the Labour Party Annual
Conference last week.

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:42.180
As you mentioned, Richard,
that will see a version

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:44.190
of the GIRFT Further Faster programme

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:47.730
targeted at 20 acute Trust-serving areas

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:50.370
with higher levels of economic inactivity

00:24:50.370 --> 00:24:53.160
and whose waiting lists show potential

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:56.490
for targeted improvement
work to increase activity

00:24:56.490 --> 00:25:00.180
and to bring waiting times down faster.

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:03.000
So on top of supporting
the efforts of all systems

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:04.830
which everybody is doing at the moment

00:25:04.830 --> 00:25:07.650
to speed up waiting
times for planned care,

00:25:07.650 --> 00:25:10.170
that is a really welcome
opportunity for the NHS

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:12.210
to demonstrate how a clinically led,

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:15.450
data-driven, patient-centered
approach to improvement

00:25:15.450 --> 00:25:19.890
can deliver real, wide-reaching
benefits for individuals

00:25:19.890 --> 00:25:22.653
and for local communities and economies.

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:26.670
We've also had, and Richard
you've mentioned this as well,

00:25:26.670 --> 00:25:29.580
but the milestone report from Lord Darzi

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:31.710
on the state of the NHS.

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:33.093
Clearly as you said,

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:36.810
a very difficult report for
anyone working in the NHS

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:40.320
to read but one that those
of us working in the NHS

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:44.610
I think would just say we
would absolutely recognise,

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:46.800
accept and endorse

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:50.100
as a diagnostic of the
current performance challenges

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:51.240
NHS teams are managing

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:53.460
and patients are experiencing

00:25:53.460 --> 00:25:55.893
and the drivers behind it.

00:25:56.760 --> 00:25:59.340
And importantly, it reiterates

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:02.400
that the NHS model is the right one,

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:05.520
that its greatest strength is its people,

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:07.230
that there are many good things

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:10.260
that we can point to
and build on in the NHS,

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:14.490
but to quote, waiting times
can and must improve quickly

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:17.250
but it will take years rather than months

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:20.433
to get the health service
back to peak performance.

00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:23.460
But if I may, just to put
my sort of personal views

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:24.390
on the record,

00:26:24.390 --> 00:26:26.910
I would share Lord Darzi's optimism

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:28.950
that despite the extent of the challenge,

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:32.790
with the right support we can
return to peak performance

00:26:32.790 --> 00:26:35.790
and with the opportunity
of the 10-year health plan,

00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:39.873
together we can make the
NHS fit for the future.

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:42.060
And on that,

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:44.700
so discussions with
Sally Warren and her team

00:26:44.700 --> 00:26:46.320
have continued since our last meeting

00:26:46.320 --> 00:26:49.230
including on the shape
of the engagement process

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:52.170
with staff, patients and the public.

00:26:52.170 --> 00:26:56.610
Where we can though we're
obviously continuing to act now

00:26:56.610 --> 00:26:58.590
to put in place the foundations

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:00.210
for the future of the NHS

00:27:00.210 --> 00:27:03.933
and as Richard, as you mentioned
a couple of times already,

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:08.310
the coverage of the
generation study this morning,

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:10.323
which is now well underway,

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:14.280
forgive me for just mentioning it again,

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:17.910
but it is genuinely a
revolutionary project

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:19.950
for the NHS in England

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:22.530
because if we can diagnose
and treat children

00:27:22.530 --> 00:27:25.830
for rare conditions, you know,
potentially years earlier,

00:27:25.830 --> 00:27:29.340
we have the potential to
stop debilitating conditions

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:30.720
in their tracks.

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:34.487
We can find difficult to
diagnose conditions earlier

00:27:34.487 --> 00:27:37.470
and we can provide earlier treatment

00:27:37.470 --> 00:27:40.470
and crucially, that will
enable more children

00:27:40.470 --> 00:27:42.840
to live happy, healthy lives,

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:45.483
starting school and living independently.

00:27:46.410 --> 00:27:49.139
So that is really important news

00:27:49.139 --> 00:27:51.570
for patients and for families.

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:53.820
But it's also important for the NHS

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:56.460
because it is another, as
you listed out, Richard,

00:27:56.460 --> 00:28:01.110
example of, you know, where
when we are able to work

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:04.530
with partners as we have in this case,

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:07.347
we really can cement the good
work that we've been doing

00:28:07.347 --> 00:28:08.550
and in this case,

00:28:08.550 --> 00:28:12.120
cement our status as a
world leader in genomics.

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:14.130
And as we look to the 10-year health plan,

00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:16.680
we know a big part of it has to be about

00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:19.260
how we set ourselves
up as well as possible

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:22.980
to be able to benefit from
these cutting edge technologies

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:26.973
that can so dramatically
improve patients' lives.

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:30.420
I should say, though,

00:28:30.420 --> 00:28:34.500
while we are always looking to the future,

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:37.470
we are of course always

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:39.150
one foot in the here and now

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:42.240
and focused on today's challenges.

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:44.700
So Richard, you've mentioned
the financial position.

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:47.700
Julian will add more on that shortly.

00:28:47.700 --> 00:28:51.000
But as usual at this point of the year,

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:54.030
the particular focus is
how we support colleagues

00:28:54.030 --> 00:28:55.390
like Kevin and Andy

00:28:57.240 --> 00:29:00.153
to manage the next few
months as well as possible.

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:02.640
As I left the house this morning,

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:06.360
I did think we really are just
beginning to enter winter.

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:08.790
I have to say that was very
early to get an early train

00:29:08.790 --> 00:29:10.470
since it's warmed up a lot since then.

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:13.080
But you know, winter is coming

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:16.350
and our winter letter
very much put the focus

00:29:16.350 --> 00:29:19.980
squarely on maintaining quality and safety

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:22.710
in the urgent, emergency care pathway

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:25.950
as well as delivering our
plans on headline priorities

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:28.080
including elective care.

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:30.023
We did discuss that in some detail.

00:29:30.023 --> 00:29:31.950
- We did.
- This morning

00:29:31.950 --> 00:29:34.260
but Sarah-Jane, I know you will cover that

00:29:34.260 --> 00:29:36.783
as part of your performance
report in a moment.

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:43.190
Vaccinations are just
another really crucial part

00:29:43.500 --> 00:29:46.380
of our sort of set of things that we know

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:49.710
we can do to help people
protect themselves

00:29:49.710 --> 00:29:52.890
and to help protect our staff over winter.

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:56.250
So I did just want to
say on that particularly,

00:29:56.250 --> 00:29:58.380
UKHSA figures out this week show

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:00.810
the very real threat of winter viruses.

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:05.130
There've been at least 18,000 deaths

00:30:05.130 --> 00:30:06.450
associated with flu

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:11.010
and over 19,500 deaths
associated with COVID-19

00:30:11.010 --> 00:30:14.130
over the past two winters.

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:17.610
Today, our COVID and flu vaccines begin.

00:30:17.610 --> 00:30:20.070
So if anyone is listening to this

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:23.100
who is eligible and has not yet booked in,

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:25.650
please do so without delay.

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:29.580
We've invited over 11 million
people to come forward

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:32.070
and it is absolutely vital

00:30:32.070 --> 00:30:35.310
that we do everything
we can within the NHS

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:37.860
to make it as easy as
possible for vulnerable groups

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:40.170
to get protected ahead of winter.

00:30:40.170 --> 00:30:43.680
So teams across the country
are already doing this

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:46.500
and that is everything from
sending teams into schools,

00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:50.790
to mobile units in supermarket
car parks giving jabs.

00:30:50.790 --> 00:30:53.040
So I'd like to thank
everyone who is involved

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:54.420
in that effort.

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:56.610
It really couldn't be more important.

00:30:56.610 --> 00:30:58.680
And also like to add my
thanks to those teams

00:30:58.680 --> 00:31:02.370
who've made the first ever RSV
vaccination rollout possible.

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:04.290
Our first data, I'm
sort of looking at Steve

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:05.910
'cause he is the lead for this

00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:08.130
and I'm conscious I might
be stealing your thunder

00:31:08.130 --> 00:31:08.963
when I say this,

00:31:08.963 --> 00:31:12.930
but our first data shows that
the RSV vaccination programme

00:31:12.930 --> 00:31:15.450
is already off to a flying start

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:20.220
with over half a million
people already protected.

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:23.370
The uptake is however
lower for pregnant women,

00:31:23.370 --> 00:31:25.290
which is a cause for concern

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:27.540
because research shows that the vaccine

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:29.760
can cut the risk of severe lung infection

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:34.380
by around 70% in the first
six months of a baby's life.

00:31:34.380 --> 00:31:36.240
So we will be working really closely

00:31:36.240 --> 00:31:38.280
and I'm looking at Duncan here as well

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:40.860
with maternity teams as well as GP teams

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:44.613
and other organisations to
encourage people to come forward.

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:47.460
As just a last point,

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:51.600
I want to say thank you to all colleagues

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:54.900
who continue to contribute to
our ongoing improvement work

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:58.230
under the NHS IMPACT banner.

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:01.170
Last week, so I'm looking
at Vin and Sarah-Jane

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:02.003
in particular here,

00:32:02.003 --> 00:32:04.950
but last week we published the first four

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:06.480
draft improvement guides

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:10.110
and they cover flow in emergency
care, elective surgery,

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:12.900
outpatients and medical
consultant job planning

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:16.170
and launched regional learning
and improvement networks

00:32:16.170 --> 00:32:18.393
led by Trust chief executives.

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:20.880
I think we're all clear

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:23.850
and in fact it was in Lord
Darzi's report as well

00:32:23.850 --> 00:32:27.810
that there is more that
we can do and should do

00:32:27.810 --> 00:32:30.150
to support the many, many people

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:32.820
who want to improve operational
and clinical pathways

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:35.850
and processes and are already doing so.

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:38.280
And this initiative is one of the things

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:40.890
we can and will continue to do now

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:43.230
whilst those discussions
about what we want to achieve

00:32:43.230 --> 00:32:44.943
in the future go on.

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:47.440
- Amanda, thank you.

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:49.740
I was just saying given my age actually,

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:51.780
you'll be pleased to know
I've also been contacted.

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:53.520
I've signed up for my vaccinations,

00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:55.770
which I'm having on Saturday afternoon.

00:32:55.770 --> 00:32:58.170
I responded to the message

00:32:58.170 --> 00:32:59.031
I received.
- Excellent role-modeling.

00:32:59.031 --> 00:33:00.697
- Very good, very good.

00:33:00.697 --> 00:33:02.073
And I would just also,

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:04.860
I just want to also
just express some thanks

00:33:04.860 --> 00:33:06.690
to non-exec colleagues if I might

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:10.110
because actually since
the various organisations

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:10.943
came together,

00:33:10.943 --> 00:33:12.990
so Health Education England,

00:33:12.990 --> 00:33:16.620
NHS Digital and NHS Improvement and NHSX,

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:18.540
actually the workload of the board

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:20.730
actually is very significant.

00:33:20.730 --> 00:33:22.770
And we received updates

00:33:22.770 --> 00:33:24.810
in this morning's private board

00:33:24.810 --> 00:33:27.660
from a range of committee chairs

00:33:27.660 --> 00:33:29.407
representing some of
the key points arising

00:33:29.407 --> 00:33:31.230
in those, in that committee work

00:33:31.230 --> 00:33:33.600
that had been done since
the last board meeting.

00:33:33.600 --> 00:33:34.620
So we had updates from

00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:37.260
the Data, Digital and Technology Committee

00:33:37.260 --> 00:33:39.360
and on the Workforce Training
and Education Committee

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:41.160
predominantly driving at delivery

00:33:41.160 --> 00:33:43.110
of the Long-Term Workforce Plan,

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:45.720
people in Remuneration Committee,

00:33:45.720 --> 00:33:47.580
the Audit and Risk Assurance Committee,

00:33:47.580 --> 00:33:48.870
a very necessary

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:51.690
and assurance and
governance function for us,

00:33:51.690 --> 00:33:53.880
had an update from the
Quality Committee from Robert,

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:54.713
who's now taken that.

00:33:54.713 --> 00:33:59.250
And thank you Robert for taking
on the Chair of that group.

00:33:59.250 --> 00:34:00.930
We also had an update on the chief,

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:02.820
the CFO advisory group

00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:05.730
and from the new Hospital
Programme Delivery Group.

00:34:05.730 --> 00:34:07.620
And often the work of the board actually,

00:34:07.620 --> 00:34:09.360
which happens between board meetings,

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:11.970
is perhaps not recognised
in terms of the demands

00:34:11.970 --> 00:34:12.867
that it places on colleagues.

00:34:12.867 --> 00:34:14.664
And my thanks to all of you

00:34:14.664 --> 00:34:16.740
and for all the work that you are doing.

00:34:16.740 --> 00:34:19.230
Now at that point, we're
going to move I think

00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:23.520
Sarah-Jane to an operations update,

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:25.740
performance update,
operational performance.

00:34:25.740 --> 00:34:27.120
Can I hand to you?

00:34:27.120 --> 00:34:28.440
- Yeah, you absolutely can.

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:29.370
Thank you, Chair.

00:34:29.370 --> 00:34:31.770
So the report I'm going to share now

00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:35.820
covers the period across July and August

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:37.830
and is obviously based
on the published data

00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:39.360
that's available.

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:41.580
Before I go into the updates,

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:43.890
I just want to reiterate Amanda's thanks

00:34:43.890 --> 00:34:47.100
for all the hard work that
is going on at the moment.

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:51.090
Just there's daily challenges
for teams right across the NHS

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:52.890
and social care indeed

00:34:52.890 --> 00:34:54.990
to deliver the standards of care

00:34:54.990 --> 00:34:57.030
that our patients need and deserve.

00:34:57.030 --> 00:34:58.680
And at the same time, we're also planning

00:34:58.680 --> 00:35:00.900
for what we know will
be a challenging winter

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:02.770
whenever flu, COVID and

00:35:04.110 --> 00:35:06.803
many of the other seasonal
illnesses present themselves.

00:35:06.803 --> 00:35:09.753
The NHS is working very
hard to get ready for that.

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:14.070
Each region is actually
having a focused session

00:35:14.070 --> 00:35:17.130
bringing together ICB and Trust colleagues

00:35:17.130 --> 00:35:18.900
to think these issues through,

00:35:18.900 --> 00:35:22.200
particularly the areas
that potentially pose

00:35:22.200 --> 00:35:25.020
the biggest risk in
those individual regions.

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:26.580
And looking at a range of areas,

00:35:26.580 --> 00:35:30.060
things like what we can do
to minimise handover delays,

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:32.280
long waits for patients

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:34.470
with mental health conditions
waiting to be moved

00:35:34.470 --> 00:35:37.200
to inpatient care,

00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:39.420
some of the in-hospital
processes and issues

00:35:39.420 --> 00:35:40.680
related to discharge.

00:35:40.680 --> 00:35:43.770
So those events have already
taken place in London

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:45.420
and the South East and
we are looking forward

00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:47.970
to more of those to follow

00:35:47.970 --> 00:35:50.220
over the coming weeks as well.

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:54.120
We did write out on the 16th of September

00:35:54.120 --> 00:35:56.640
just to really reiterate
that which was already

00:35:56.640 --> 00:36:00.990
in the UEC Recovery Plan
One Year On document,

00:36:00.990 --> 00:36:02.610
just really highlighting the things

00:36:02.610 --> 00:36:05.970
that can help all systems
to prepare for winter

00:36:05.970 --> 00:36:08.790
and just again, highlighting
the good practise

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:11.100
and the interventions that
we know can make a difference

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:13.860
even in really challenging circumstances.

00:36:13.860 --> 00:36:16.140
And the guides that
Amanda has just alluded to

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:17.490
really go to support that

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:22.490
and further codify the steps
that organisations can take.

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:25.080
We're also really pleased that
since we last met together

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:25.913
as a board,

00:36:25.913 --> 00:36:27.000
the junior doctors have accepted

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:28.560
the government's pay offer,

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:31.410
which is really great news
for everybody across the NHS,

00:36:31.410 --> 00:36:33.180
but for us in operations,

00:36:33.180 --> 00:36:35.820
it gives us certainty
about our winter planning.

00:36:35.820 --> 00:36:39.000
That was perhaps more
difficult for us last year

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:41.250
in terms of really being
able to shore up our

00:36:42.120 --> 00:36:43.800
rotas and approach.

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:47.160
So I'm going to run through the headlines.

00:36:47.160 --> 00:36:49.800
I think in all of the areas
there are common themes,

00:36:49.800 --> 00:36:51.240
there are huge challenges.

00:36:51.240 --> 00:36:53.250
We know the needs of our population.

00:36:53.250 --> 00:36:55.590
There's some really impressive numbers,

00:36:55.590 --> 00:36:57.450
but I wouldn't want to give the impression

00:36:57.450 --> 00:36:58.920
in presenting them that we know

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:01.900
that there's not a huge challenge
that sits alongside that

00:37:02.867 --> 00:37:05.220
and that there's still
a lot further to go.

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:07.890
And just as Lord Darzi's
report makes clear,

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:09.240
I think we're all really determined

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:11.040
to step into that challenge.

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:16.040
So just in terms of UEC
performance in August,

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:21.290
we achieved 76.3% for emergency
department performance,

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:27.120
which is the highest I
think for around 18 months.

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:30.480
It was again against a
backdrop of increased demand

00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:33.270
from that which we
normally see in the summer,

00:37:33.270 --> 00:37:36.410
and we delivered a category
two ambulance response time

00:37:36.410 --> 00:37:40.320
of 27 minutes and 25 seconds.

00:37:40.320 --> 00:37:43.953
So again, that was a significant
improvement on previously.

00:37:45.330 --> 00:37:47.520
We obviously, not complacent about that.

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:50.040
We always do better in the August period.

00:37:50.040 --> 00:37:51.150
We know that things have become

00:37:51.150 --> 00:37:52.947
a little bit more
challenging in September,

00:37:52.947 --> 00:37:56.460
but I think it does prove
that when we are focused

00:37:56.460 --> 00:37:59.280
on our interventions and demand
is at a reasonable level,

00:37:59.280 --> 00:38:01.350
we are able to provide
that level of service

00:38:01.350 --> 00:38:02.183
to our patients,

00:38:02.183 --> 00:38:03.123
albeit more to do.

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:06.360
In terms of electives,

00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:08.700
the total waiting list increased

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:12.900
just for the second time
since October, 2023,

00:38:12.900 --> 00:38:17.640
but only a small increase
of 1,651 patients.

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:19.980
Our main focus has been seeing

00:38:19.980 --> 00:38:22.380
as many patients as we possibly can,

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:23.790
which has absolutely happened.

00:38:23.790 --> 00:38:27.300
Our activity levels are at a record high

00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:29.760
and also focusing on those
long waiting patients.

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:32.250
We've got the milestone
that we are working towards

00:38:32.250 --> 00:38:36.960
for the end of September in
terms of the 65-week waits.

00:38:36.960 --> 00:38:39.300
So we will report on that
at the next board meeting.

00:38:39.300 --> 00:38:43.050
But everyone across the
NHS is focused on that.

00:38:43.050 --> 00:38:46.830
In terms of cancer, again,
we've seen high referral rates

00:38:46.830 --> 00:38:51.570
but we've maintained good
progress against the standards

00:38:51.570 --> 00:38:53.970
and we are continuing to monitor closely.

00:38:53.970 --> 00:38:56.520
Obviously the faster
diagnosis standard remains

00:38:56.520 --> 00:38:58.917
that which we keep very close attention on

00:38:58.917 --> 00:39:02.670
and that stood at 76.2% in July,

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:05.310
which is good performance
against the standard.

00:39:05.310 --> 00:39:08.220
In diagnostics, again, our activity levels

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:10.230
are up significantly

00:39:10.230 --> 00:39:12.360
and that's in large part due to

00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:13.980
the Community Diagnostic Centres,

00:39:13.980 --> 00:39:17.190
which we talked about at the
private board this morning.

00:39:17.190 --> 00:39:19.260
But again, demand for diagnostic services

00:39:19.260 --> 00:39:21.990
both in planned and unscheduled care

00:39:21.990 --> 00:39:24.570
is also continuing to increase.

00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:26.310
So whilst we are making improvements

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:28.740
against the six-week wait,

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:30.660
the size of the waiting list is growing.

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:32.700
So I think diagnostics is an area of focus

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:36.030
that we want to really
understand moving forward,

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:37.770
particularly some of the growing demands

00:39:37.770 --> 00:39:41.610
that we're seeing from, for
diagnostics in unscheduled care.

00:39:41.610 --> 00:39:43.683
And then for mental health,

00:39:44.790 --> 00:39:46.650
this is June and July data just to,

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:49.050
based on the way that
mental health data flows.

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:54.050
The dementia diagnosis rate
is at 65.2% as of July, 2024

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:57.870
against our ambition of 66.7.

00:39:57.870 --> 00:39:59.700
So we've got a little
bit further to go there,

00:39:59.700 --> 00:40:02.070
but it is continued steady improvement

00:40:02.070 --> 00:40:06.360
since the standard was
introduced in January, 2023.

00:40:06.360 --> 00:40:10.230
We've also seen some good
improvements in adults

00:40:10.230 --> 00:40:12.720
completing their course of treatment

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:14.430
and children and young people's access

00:40:14.430 --> 00:40:15.870
to mental health services

00:40:15.870 --> 00:40:18.774
and perinatal mental health services.

00:40:18.774 --> 00:40:21.780
Although again, there's lots
of focus across the regions

00:40:21.780 --> 00:40:25.110
to make sure that we absolutely
deliver to those standards.

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:28.920
I think the one area that
we are jointly working on

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:31.800
between the UEC and
the mental health teams

00:40:31.800 --> 00:40:34.200
are those urgent and
emergency care services

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:35.880
for mental health patients.

00:40:35.880 --> 00:40:39.780
Where despite all of the
efforts to do everything we can

00:40:39.780 --> 00:40:43.200
to maintain flow across
our mental health pathways,

00:40:43.200 --> 00:40:45.390
we are still seeing too many patients

00:40:45.390 --> 00:40:47.280
that are having to wait unacceptably long

00:40:47.280 --> 00:40:49.410
in our emergency departments

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:52.140
and in some of our acute hospitals

00:40:52.140 --> 00:40:55.680
waiting for inpatient
beds to be available,

00:40:55.680 --> 00:40:58.530
which in turn is often as
a result of our challenges

00:40:58.530 --> 00:41:01.020
moving people back into the community

00:41:01.020 --> 00:41:03.000
after their inpatient mental health stay

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:04.800
and getting them the right housing

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:07.560
and social care support as well.

00:41:07.560 --> 00:41:08.393
So

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:11.880
I'll conclude as I started,

00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:14.460
some brilliant work
going on across the NHS,

00:41:14.460 --> 00:41:16.770
some really impressive numbers,

00:41:16.770 --> 00:41:19.230
but I think we are really aware
that for all those patients

00:41:19.230 --> 00:41:21.030
that we have been able to look after

00:41:21.030 --> 00:41:22.200
in the way we want,

00:41:22.200 --> 00:41:24.240
there are big groups of
people that are still waiting

00:41:24.240 --> 00:41:25.620
for access to our care

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:27.510
and we need to remain absolutely focused

00:41:27.510 --> 00:41:31.020
both on delivering the
standards for this year

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:32.670
but also now with an eye to the future

00:41:32.670 --> 00:41:34.590
to think about how we
can continue to bring

00:41:34.590 --> 00:41:35.847
some of those waiting times down

00:41:35.847 --> 00:41:40.470
and access for patients across
all services in a timely way.

00:41:40.470 --> 00:41:41.303
Thank you.

00:41:41.303 --> 00:41:42.136
- Sarah-Jane, thank you.

00:41:42.136 --> 00:41:45.390
Let me pause and see if
colleagues have any comments

00:41:45.390 --> 00:41:46.380
or questions for you.

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:49.293
So what, let's go Wol then Andrew.

00:41:51.390 --> 00:41:54.810
- Sarah-Jane thanks and
yeah, it looks good,

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:56.850
but as we're going into winter,

00:41:56.850 --> 00:41:59.490
is it worth just touching on your view

00:41:59.490 --> 00:42:01.380
of the state of the system

00:42:01.380 --> 00:42:03.990
as we're going into winter and
the resilience in the system?

00:42:03.990 --> 00:42:05.340
Because I know you and
colleagues have been doing

00:42:05.340 --> 00:42:07.173
a lot of work on the winter plan.

00:42:08.520 --> 00:42:11.550
- Yeah, so I think we are very concerned

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:13.920
about the resilience of the
system as we look from here.

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:17.013
I mean, it is different in
different places in the country,

00:42:18.690 --> 00:42:21.990
but even in our best performers

00:42:21.990 --> 00:42:24.000
we are in a position where it only takes

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:25.680
a small change in something,

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:30.120
just a bit of a surge of
need for whatever reason.

00:42:30.120 --> 00:42:31.887
And then some of our
flow can really silt up

00:42:31.887 --> 00:42:35.760
and what happens as soon as
one part of that flow silts up,

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:38.850
then it becomes a problem
all the way down the line.

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:42.090
So one of the things that
we've been focused on is

00:42:42.090 --> 00:42:44.190
what is it that we can do,

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:46.710
things that we can control
ourselves inside the NHS?

00:42:46.710 --> 00:42:49.260
There's many other societal
factors here as we know

00:42:49.260 --> 00:42:51.120
and sort of the relationship
with social care.

00:42:51.120 --> 00:42:53.040
But what can we absolutely focus on

00:42:53.040 --> 00:42:56.100
in terms of the interventions
that we know work

00:42:56.100 --> 00:42:58.620
and using our GIRFT and our ESIS teams

00:42:58.620 --> 00:43:00.090
to support those organisations

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:02.460
where we most worried about
safety and experience,

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:05.130
which is what we have been doing.

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:07.350
We also have been doing additional work

00:43:07.350 --> 00:43:09.270
in terms of our operational response.

00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:11.550
So if when we do get into those situations

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:14.310
where waits are unacceptably long,

00:43:14.310 --> 00:43:16.380
how do we safely make choices

00:43:16.380 --> 00:43:18.053
to balance risk across the system?

00:43:18.053 --> 00:43:20.103
I know this is a discussion every winter,

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:22.540
but I think we are concerned

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:26.190
and one of the things that
we don't have this winter

00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:28.440
that we did have in the previous winter

00:43:28.440 --> 00:43:30.570
is some of that additional capacity.

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:33.120
We are really reliant on
our process improvements

00:43:33.120 --> 00:43:34.380
and our interventions this year,

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:37.320
which as I say, there's
been fantastic progress on,

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:40.470
but we don't have that
ability to surge our beds

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:42.603
much higher than they already are.

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:44.820
We don't have additional money

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:48.243
to support social care
discharge like we had,

00:43:49.262 --> 00:43:50.820
like we've had in previous years.

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:53.160
So I think, yeah, it's,

00:43:53.160 --> 00:43:54.810
we're doing everything we can to prepare,

00:43:54.810 --> 00:43:56.490
but I think it will be challenging

00:43:56.490 --> 00:43:59.196
in particular places at particular times.

00:43:59.196 --> 00:44:02.010
- Thank you, Andrew then Jane and then,

00:44:02.010 --> 00:44:04.080
Sarah-Jane if you, we'll
gather those questions up

00:44:04.080 --> 00:44:06.341
and then if you pick 'em up as a,

00:44:06.341 --> 00:44:07.853
either a batch or as a
couple, that'd be great.

00:44:07.853 --> 00:44:08.740
Go ahead, Andrew.

00:44:08.740 --> 00:44:11.130
- So if I recall correctly,

00:44:11.130 --> 00:44:14.680
last year was the first year since 2015

00:44:14.680 --> 00:44:17.460
where performance against
the constitutional standards

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:19.050
did not deteriorate,

00:44:19.050 --> 00:44:21.033
which was really good news.

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:27.080
So big emphasis this year
clearly on betterment,

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:31.290
to try and get patients
through as quickly as possible.

00:44:31.290 --> 00:44:34.110
Just to get your reflections
would be good, Sarah-Jane,

00:44:34.110 --> 00:44:37.980
on 12 hours 'cause that for
me is the real acid test

00:44:37.980 --> 00:44:39.160
around flow

00:44:40.050 --> 00:44:45.050
and to what extent do you
think we can further improve

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:47.970
our performance against
that particular standard.

00:44:47.970 --> 00:44:49.590
And the other one is on mental health,

00:44:49.590 --> 00:44:52.950
where I thought every acute hospital

00:44:52.950 --> 00:44:55.230
had got a liaison service in place

00:44:55.230 --> 00:44:56.940
that's seven days a week.

00:44:56.940 --> 00:44:59.728
Is that still correct?

00:44:59.728 --> 00:45:01.770
- Can you hold those
and we'll just pick up

00:45:01.770 --> 00:45:03.273
any comments that Jane, do?

00:45:04.290 --> 00:45:07.860
- Yeah, two slightly different
comments or questions.

00:45:07.860 --> 00:45:11.250
One, just sort of thinking
ahead particularly to winter,

00:45:11.250 --> 00:45:16.250
the report mentions a good
uptaking of Pharmacy First,

00:45:16.800 --> 00:45:18.117
but just interested to know

00:45:18.117 --> 00:45:20.647
and it says it's sort of
continuing to pick up,

00:45:20.647 --> 00:45:24.120
just wondering how fast
that pickup is going,

00:45:24.120 --> 00:45:26.377
whether there's a plan to accelerate it,

00:45:26.377 --> 00:45:28.530
you know, whether that would make

00:45:28.530 --> 00:45:32.160
a helpful marginal difference
in different systems?

00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:34.740
So just wanted to understand
a little bit more about that.

00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:38.340
And then the other point
is sort of a bit sort of

00:45:38.340 --> 00:45:41.550
into the detail of the report
around longer-term conditions

00:45:41.550 --> 00:45:42.750
and prevention.

00:45:42.750 --> 00:45:45.900
There's an interesting
piece around the pilots

00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:47.790
on blood pressure check services

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:50.640
in wider primary care settings

00:45:50.640 --> 00:45:53.520
and it says that's going to
run till summer next year

00:45:53.520 --> 00:45:57.780
and then be evaluated and
report by the end of August.

00:45:57.780 --> 00:45:59.400
I suppose I'm just interested,

00:45:59.400 --> 00:46:00.780
particularly thinking to a link

00:46:00.780 --> 00:46:02.880
to the Health and Work Agenda,

00:46:02.880 --> 00:46:04.170
whether that,

00:46:04.170 --> 00:46:05.820
how that will be evaluated

00:46:05.820 --> 00:46:08.350
and then how quickly
that could be stepped up

00:46:09.300 --> 00:46:11.020
if that evaluation is

00:46:12.270 --> 00:46:15.300
sort of seen to be, you know, successful.

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:16.770
- Do you want to have a go
at those then I'll bring in,

00:46:16.770 --> 00:46:17.787
I know Robert wants to come in

00:46:17.787 --> 00:46:20.430
- If it's okay to ask Amanda
to pick up the ones from Jane,

00:46:20.430 --> 00:46:21.263
if that's okay.

00:46:21.263 --> 00:46:23.910
Amanda Doyle, if that's, if that,

00:46:23.910 --> 00:46:26.100
but on the two, on the 12 hours, yeah,

00:46:26.100 --> 00:46:30.870
I mean that I think is the
most important of the metrics

00:46:30.870 --> 00:46:33.270
to focus on from a safety perspective.

00:46:33.270 --> 00:46:36.060
And we haven't made as many
inroads into the 12 hours

00:46:36.060 --> 00:46:41.060
as we have in the four hours
and the Cat 2 response times.

00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:43.920
It is the focus of our activities

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:46.350
when we are working with
systems from an NHS perspective.

00:46:46.350 --> 00:46:48.270
So the ones that we have selected

00:46:48.270 --> 00:46:51.450
to give additional support
to in the run-up to winter,

00:46:51.450 --> 00:46:53.130
it's been really around the 12 hours

00:46:53.130 --> 00:46:55.590
and the overcrowding in the department,

00:46:55.590 --> 00:46:57.120
and as you know well, Andrew,

00:46:57.120 --> 00:46:58.920
that is really linked to what is going on

00:46:58.920 --> 00:47:00.000
in the broader hospital

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:02.280
and our abilities to be
able to move patients

00:47:02.280 --> 00:47:04.140
through the system.

00:47:04.140 --> 00:47:06.900
I think one of the
things that we've noticed

00:47:06.900 --> 00:47:08.520
in quite a lot of the Trusts
that we've worked with,

00:47:08.520 --> 00:47:10.680
although there are clearly issues

00:47:10.680 --> 00:47:12.780
with discharging patients
out into community

00:47:12.780 --> 00:47:14.580
and social care services,

00:47:14.580 --> 00:47:17.100
there are things between
the emergency department,

00:47:17.100 --> 00:47:20.640
the acute physicians and the
specialties that we can do.

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:23.700
So we will continue to focus
and keep going with it.

00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:26.730
But yeah, I agree it's, you know,

00:47:26.730 --> 00:47:30.210
it is the metric and we need
to be very, very focused.

00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:32.790
In terms of liaison psychiatry, yes,

00:47:32.790 --> 00:47:33.850
that is in place

00:47:35.490 --> 00:47:40.050
across hospitals and we've,

00:47:40.050 --> 00:47:41.100
all the work that we've done

00:47:41.100 --> 00:47:44.160
between the mental health and
the emergency department teams

00:47:44.160 --> 00:47:47.010
are saying that patients
are having those assessments

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:49.413
and plans put in place in a timely way,

00:47:50.250 --> 00:47:52.890
and the majority of time that's working.

00:47:52.890 --> 00:47:55.230
The issue becomes when
it's really clear to that

00:47:55.230 --> 00:47:56.880
liaison psychiatry team that

00:47:56.880 --> 00:47:58.200
that patient is going to need

00:47:58.200 --> 00:48:00.510
an inpatient mental health bed.

00:48:00.510 --> 00:48:02.580
And whilst we can initiate some treatments

00:48:02.580 --> 00:48:05.430
in an emergency department or
an acute hospital environment,

00:48:05.430 --> 00:48:07.050
it's very difficult to really start

00:48:07.050 --> 00:48:09.240
the therapeutic pathways.

00:48:09.240 --> 00:48:11.127
So those services are working

00:48:11.127 --> 00:48:13.290
and again we've got learning
between the, you know,

00:48:13.290 --> 00:48:15.900
everywhere to check we're
all doing it in the best way

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:20.910
but we still need to get
patients to the right place

00:48:20.910 --> 00:48:23.310
and we just can't always do that.

00:48:23.310 --> 00:48:24.390
- We need, you might,

00:48:24.390 --> 00:48:26.610
when we come to that
paper pick up on that,

00:48:26.610 --> 00:48:28.680
if you want to add to that point at all.

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:29.876
It was Amanda.
- Can I predict

00:48:29.876 --> 00:48:31.830
the Pharmacy First and the.

00:48:31.830 --> 00:48:36.510
- So as you know, we had
really enthusiastic adoption

00:48:36.510 --> 00:48:38.550
of Pharmacy First delivery from pharmacies

00:48:38.550 --> 00:48:40.110
across the country.

00:48:40.110 --> 00:48:42.690
Very, very high proportions of pharmacies.

00:48:42.690 --> 00:48:45.120
It was planned to have
a gradually increasing

00:48:45.120 --> 00:48:47.310
trajectory of numbers,

00:48:47.310 --> 00:48:49.890
partly because pharmacies
need to establish the systems

00:48:49.890 --> 00:48:52.770
but also it relies very
much on a change in patient

00:48:52.770 --> 00:48:54.330
and GP behaviour.

00:48:54.330 --> 00:48:56.013
And so we had to embed that.

00:48:56.850 --> 00:48:58.803
That continues to grow.

00:49:00.270 --> 00:49:02.850
A lot of the conditions that are covered

00:49:02.850 --> 00:49:04.590
by the Common Conditions Services

00:49:04.590 --> 00:49:07.440
are more seasonal conditions,
so minor infections.

00:49:07.440 --> 00:49:09.630
And so we're expecting
another growth over winter

00:49:09.630 --> 00:49:12.060
and have accommodated that

00:49:12.060 --> 00:49:14.940
so that patients starting
to get sore throats,

00:49:14.940 --> 00:49:17.733
coughs, et cetera, can access pharmacy.

00:49:18.840 --> 00:49:23.250
And we are evaluating
it so early, you know,

00:49:23.250 --> 00:49:25.470
teething troubles around IT, for example,

00:49:25.470 --> 00:49:26.303
are being

00:49:27.780 --> 00:49:30.150
resolved as we go along.

00:49:30.150 --> 00:49:33.780
And the evaluation around
things like use of antibiotics

00:49:33.780 --> 00:49:36.570
for purposes of avoiding
antimicrobial resistance

00:49:36.570 --> 00:49:37.403
are ongoing.

00:49:38.370 --> 00:49:39.990
As far as blood pressure checking

00:49:39.990 --> 00:49:41.520
in wider primary care settings,

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:43.590
we already have a very well established,

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:48.590
very strongly performing
programme of blood pressure checks

00:49:48.840 --> 00:49:50.040
in pharmacies

00:49:50.040 --> 00:49:53.700
and what this pilot is doing
is looking at widening that out

00:49:53.700 --> 00:49:55.470
to people like dentists.

00:49:55.470 --> 00:49:57.210
It's only just started,

00:49:57.210 --> 00:49:58.860
but the plan is to evaluate that.

00:49:58.860 --> 00:50:01.170
Any opportunity to check blood pressure

00:50:01.170 --> 00:50:04.200
and identify untreated hypertension

00:50:04.200 --> 00:50:07.350
is a really good prevention opportunity.

00:50:07.350 --> 00:50:08.220
- Thank you, Amanda.

00:50:08.220 --> 00:50:11.010
Robert, I think you had
a comment or question.

00:50:11.010 --> 00:50:16.010
- Yeah, so the improvement
in cancer early diagnosis

00:50:17.100 --> 00:50:18.390
is encouraging

00:50:18.390 --> 00:50:19.740
and it's not where it needs to be yet,

00:50:19.740 --> 00:50:21.690
but it's certainly an encouraging trend

00:50:22.530 --> 00:50:25.890
and rapid access to diagnostics is key

00:50:25.890 --> 00:50:28.890
to pursuing that positive trend.

00:50:28.890 --> 00:50:31.710
Community Diagnostic Centres,
I'm sure a part of that.

00:50:31.710 --> 00:50:33.180
My question is this,

00:50:33.180 --> 00:50:37.620
so I think direct referral
by GPs for diagnostics

00:50:37.620 --> 00:50:39.270
is something I'm very much in favour of,

00:50:39.270 --> 00:50:40.800
but one of the anxieties about it

00:50:40.800 --> 00:50:44.430
is that it's amplifying
demand, demand is going up.

00:50:44.430 --> 00:50:48.030
Can you comment on whether direct referral

00:50:48.030 --> 00:50:50.040
is part of the increase in demand

00:50:50.040 --> 00:50:51.240
or is it just the increase in demand

00:50:51.240 --> 00:50:52.380
because there's an increase in demand?

00:50:52.380 --> 00:50:54.270
- I think we are seeing increase in demand

00:50:54.270 --> 00:50:56.310
from all modalities

00:50:56.310 --> 00:50:57.160
and I think that,

00:50:58.290 --> 00:51:00.240
I think that's why we need
to just explore a little bit

00:51:00.240 --> 00:51:01.920
and understand a bit more what is going on

00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:04.650
because it might be that
this is entirely appropriate

00:51:04.650 --> 00:51:07.680
and that getting that fast
access to the, you know,

00:51:07.680 --> 00:51:10.380
that is preventing from
patients from pursuing

00:51:10.380 --> 00:51:11.883
other parts of pathways.

00:51:13.080 --> 00:51:15.030
But yeah, I think we need
to come back together

00:51:15.030 --> 00:51:17.430
and explore this in more detail.

00:51:17.430 --> 00:51:18.263
- Brilliant.

00:51:18.263 --> 00:51:19.710
- Shall I just make a comment to that?

00:51:19.710 --> 00:51:24.660
In the past where single
diagnostics have been rolled out

00:51:24.660 --> 00:51:27.540
to GPs where they
previously weren't available

00:51:27.540 --> 00:51:30.120
in lots of different places over years,

00:51:30.120 --> 00:51:32.880
evaluations have shown
that it doesn't increase

00:51:32.880 --> 00:51:34.992
the number of.
- That's what I hoped, yeah.

00:51:34.992 --> 00:51:36.990
- Inappropriate investigations.

00:51:36.990 --> 00:51:39.510
- Often GPs were referring to acute trusts

00:51:39.510 --> 00:51:41.520
simply to gain access to a diagnostic.

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:42.353
- Exactly, yeah.

00:51:43.200 --> 00:51:44.340
- Thank you.

00:51:44.340 --> 00:51:46.500
If there are no other
comments, Sarah-Jane,

00:51:46.500 --> 00:51:48.330
thank you very much.

00:51:48.330 --> 00:51:49.950
Julian, can we hand to you

00:51:49.950 --> 00:51:52.053
for the latest financial position?

00:51:53.280 --> 00:51:54.630
- Sure.

00:51:54.630 --> 00:51:57.753
So at the risk of sounding
like a broken record,

00:52:00.300 --> 00:52:04.230
I've said the reality is
that this year will feel

00:52:04.230 --> 00:52:06.813
much tougher than most.

00:52:08.310 --> 00:52:10.920
There's no doubt the finances
are going to be challenging

00:52:10.920 --> 00:52:15.920
and we're asking a lot from
systems, ICBs and providers.

00:52:17.820 --> 00:52:20.700
They need to deliver an unprecedented

00:52:20.700 --> 00:52:24.180
circa 9 billion pounds
of annual efficiencies,

00:52:24.180 --> 00:52:27.075
which on one measure is about sort of 7%

00:52:27.075 --> 00:52:28.923
of their total allocations.

00:52:29.940 --> 00:52:31.443
So that is a big ask.

00:52:32.310 --> 00:52:33.930
Although it's tough,

00:52:33.930 --> 00:52:36.030
I don't think it is impossible

00:52:36.030 --> 00:52:38.850
and it is a testament to the hard work

00:52:38.850 --> 00:52:41.040
that people are putting in,

00:52:41.040 --> 00:52:45.330
managers, staff, across the board

00:52:45.330 --> 00:52:48.120
that this year they've already made

00:52:48.120 --> 00:52:50.520
to the end of month four
2 billion pounds worth

00:52:50.520 --> 00:52:52.860
of efficiency savings

00:52:52.860 --> 00:52:57.150
and that is 300 million
more than at the same point

00:52:57.150 --> 00:52:59.010
last year.

00:52:59.010 --> 00:53:02.970
And when you look at acute
sector productivity year-on-year

00:53:02.970 --> 00:53:04.200
to the end of month four,

00:53:04.200 --> 00:53:08.700
we are about 2.8 percentage points up

00:53:08.700 --> 00:53:10.167
on '23-'24

00:53:10.167 --> 00:53:12.600
and you can see sort of
a, sort of steady growth

00:53:12.600 --> 00:53:15.420
over the course of the first few months.

00:53:15.420 --> 00:53:17.790
So a lot of good work going on.

00:53:17.790 --> 00:53:19.840
You look at agency spending

00:53:21.450 --> 00:53:23.310
as a percentage of total pay costs,

00:53:23.310 --> 00:53:24.780
it's the lowest it's ever been.

00:53:24.780 --> 00:53:27.420
Well, certainly since about 2017,

00:53:27.420 --> 00:53:29.760
which is the earliest we have data for.

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:30.593
And

00:53:34.217 --> 00:53:38.490
the NHS is on track to reduce
the gross spend on agency

00:53:38.490 --> 00:53:40.800
by 500 million pounds
by the end of this year.

00:53:40.800 --> 00:53:43.470
So real work going on,

00:53:43.470 --> 00:53:46.060
real savings being made

00:53:48.420 --> 00:53:51.240
and that is against a backdrop.

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:55.320
Whereas Sarah-Jane has just explained,

00:53:55.320 --> 00:53:58.620
we are making progress on performance,

00:53:58.620 --> 00:54:00.180
though it is tough

00:54:00.180 --> 00:54:01.680
and you know,

00:54:01.680 --> 00:54:04.830
no one is claiming we
are where we want to be

00:54:04.830 --> 00:54:06.420
but we are holding things together

00:54:06.420 --> 00:54:10.360
and making progress in almost every area

00:54:11.340 --> 00:54:14.253
including, you know,
falling waiting times.

00:54:15.780 --> 00:54:18.900
Now as the paper says, the,

00:54:18.900 --> 00:54:19.980
despite that,

00:54:19.980 --> 00:54:21.900
the aggregate system position shows

00:54:21.900 --> 00:54:26.610
we are above plan at the end of month four

00:54:26.610 --> 00:54:29.820
for the NHS at the kind of
NHS England mandate level,

00:54:29.820 --> 00:54:33.450
we are 387 million pounds above plan.

00:54:33.450 --> 00:54:35.100
But within systems themselves,

00:54:35.100 --> 00:54:38.970
they're 487 million pounds above plan.

00:54:38.970 --> 00:54:42.400
And that is in part driven
by industrial action

00:54:44.190 --> 00:54:46.680
where, you know, using similar estimates

00:54:46.680 --> 00:54:47.850
to the ones we used last year,

00:54:47.850 --> 00:54:50.553
that accounts for about
180 million pounds of that,

00:54:52.560 --> 00:54:56.100
of that 487.

00:54:56.100 --> 00:54:57.520
And we are ahead

00:54:58.680 --> 00:55:01.290
of where, you know,

00:55:01.290 --> 00:55:02.790
we were this time last year

00:55:02.790 --> 00:55:05.280
when things looked equally, you know,

00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:07.623
were equally tough.

00:55:09.480 --> 00:55:12.930
So, and we have secured extra funding

00:55:12.930 --> 00:55:15.720
to cover those industrial action costs,

00:55:15.720 --> 00:55:17.340
which we have

00:55:17.340 --> 00:55:19.830
or are in the process of giving to systems

00:55:19.830 --> 00:55:22.230
along with

00:55:22.230 --> 00:55:24.630
the funding to cover the pay deals

00:55:24.630 --> 00:55:28.560
that have been agreed by the government

00:55:28.560 --> 00:55:30.603
and with unions.

00:55:31.470 --> 00:55:32.303
So

00:55:35.310 --> 00:55:37.593
it is hard, we're behind plan.

00:55:40.020 --> 00:55:44.550
Really important to say that, you know,

00:55:44.550 --> 00:55:46.680
as I said at the beginning of the year,

00:55:46.680 --> 00:55:51.150
you know, we really do
not have any, you know,

00:55:51.150 --> 00:55:52.410
sort of real contingency

00:55:52.410 --> 00:55:55.110
and even to get to the
point we have got to,

00:55:55.110 --> 00:55:57.720
it's this is not free money, you know?

00:55:57.720 --> 00:56:01.470
We have had to limit
spending on other piece,

00:56:01.470 --> 00:56:03.912
on other programmes and
other pieces of work

00:56:03.912 --> 00:56:08.220
on transformation and capacity building.

00:56:08.220 --> 00:56:09.270
So, you know,

00:56:09.270 --> 00:56:12.960
this is categorically not
what we want to be doing now,

00:56:12.960 --> 00:56:16.440
particularly when you
think how we need to fix

00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:19.830
you know, things within the NHS

00:56:19.830 --> 00:56:21.900
and deal with the issues
that have been raised

00:56:21.900 --> 00:56:24.513
in Lord Darzi's investigation.

00:56:26.250 --> 00:56:27.083
Now

00:56:28.860 --> 00:56:31.140
in order to deal with this issue,

00:56:31.140 --> 00:56:32.910
as I have described in the previous board,

00:56:32.910 --> 00:56:36.810
we have strengthened our
oversight and intervention regime

00:56:36.810 --> 00:56:39.420
for the most challenged systems.

00:56:39.420 --> 00:56:40.530
And I've now had,

00:56:40.530 --> 00:56:42.600
I've met with a lot of them,

00:56:42.600 --> 00:56:44.700
we have gone through where
they are on recovery plans.

00:56:44.700 --> 00:56:46.710
We can see where they are on it,

00:56:46.710 --> 00:56:49.800
we can see where they are
still struggling to get on it.

00:56:49.800 --> 00:56:53.460
We are agreeing what
actions they need to take

00:56:53.460 --> 00:56:56.760
to get back to the plans

00:56:56.760 --> 00:57:00.674
that boards themselves agreed.

00:57:00.674 --> 00:57:01.507
- [Person Off-Camera] Yeah.
- And actually I think

00:57:01.507 --> 00:57:03.720
it's just important to labour that point.

00:57:03.720 --> 00:57:07.890
This is things boards
agreed and signed off

00:57:07.890 --> 00:57:11.190
within providers, within
Integrated Care Boards

00:57:11.190 --> 00:57:14.853
and within sort of the
integrated care system.

00:57:16.170 --> 00:57:18.930
We've been clear that there's
no further wiggle room.

00:57:18.930 --> 00:57:21.720
So whilst really good
progress has been made,

00:57:21.720 --> 00:57:23.010
there is a lot to do

00:57:23.010 --> 00:57:25.110
and there's a lot to do
as we head into the winter

00:57:25.110 --> 00:57:29.670
whilst we also are dealing
with the, you know,

00:57:29.670 --> 00:57:31.650
with winter and performance

00:57:31.650 --> 00:57:35.100
and people need to work out
how they're going to do both.

00:57:35.100 --> 00:57:39.120
But you know, that is what
the vast majority are doing

00:57:39.120 --> 00:57:43.470
and you can see that basically
half of providers are on plan

00:57:43.470 --> 00:57:45.030
as we speak today

00:57:45.030 --> 00:57:49.530
with all of the challenges
that everyone is facing.

00:57:49.530 --> 00:57:51.150
We know it's tough,

00:57:51.150 --> 00:57:55.290
but it is the reality of the
economic situation we're in

00:57:55.290 --> 00:57:57.960
and I think it's just
never been more important

00:57:57.960 --> 00:58:00.750
for systems and providers

00:58:00.750 --> 00:58:03.993
to deliver the plans that they agreed.

00:58:05.760 --> 00:58:09.520
So progress, lots to do

00:58:11.790 --> 00:58:15.060
and you know, we will work with people,

00:58:15.060 --> 00:58:18.660
support people to, you
know, do what is necessary,

00:58:18.660 --> 00:58:20.580
but in the end this,

00:58:20.580 --> 00:58:21.780
it isn't just the finance director,

00:58:21.780 --> 00:58:24.480
it's the executive team owning
what they've signed up to

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:26.393
and working out a way of making it work.

00:58:27.270 --> 00:58:28.350
- Thank you, Julian.

00:58:28.350 --> 00:58:30.287
Wol, I know you want to come in.

00:58:30.287 --> 00:58:31.140
- Just very quick for Julian,

00:58:31.140 --> 00:58:34.933
you mentioned the overspend
was around half a million,

00:58:34.933 --> 00:58:36.330
0.7 million,

00:58:36.330 --> 00:58:37.740
but if you actually look at the numbers,

00:58:37.740 --> 00:58:40.860
the expenditure was closer to a billion

00:58:40.860 --> 00:58:43.830
and some of that came back
through higher revenue.

00:58:43.830 --> 00:58:48.090
Is, could you sort of unpack
that a little bit for us?

00:58:48.090 --> 00:58:49.690
- Yes, so

00:58:51.630 --> 00:58:53.160
I mean there are always variances

00:58:53.160 --> 00:58:54.810
between expenditure and income

00:58:54.810 --> 00:58:59.810
and some of this will be even
as people get in, you know,

00:59:00.299 --> 00:59:02.820
it always depends on how they income

00:59:02.820 --> 00:59:06.870
including from like research,
commercial activities.

00:59:06.870 --> 00:59:09.810
But in part some of this is also

00:59:09.810 --> 00:59:13.230
where people are really delivering well

00:59:13.230 --> 00:59:15.573
on elective activity.

00:59:16.680 --> 00:59:20.250
I mean, compared to the
average from last year,

00:59:20.250 --> 00:59:22.830
it looks like on sort evaluated basis

00:59:22.830 --> 00:59:25.650
people are doing 5 to 6%
more than they did last year.

00:59:25.650 --> 00:59:29.340
And that is actually why
we are seeing progress

00:59:29.340 --> 00:59:30.240
on the waiting list,

00:59:30.240 --> 00:59:32.330
is why when, you know,

00:59:32.330 --> 00:59:36.810
at the end of July year-on-year,
the waiting list is lower.

00:59:36.810 --> 00:59:39.660
It's why I think we will see that continue

00:59:39.660 --> 00:59:41.700
and indeed let's hope the sort of gap

00:59:41.700 --> 00:59:45.120
between last year and this year,
you know, sort of opens up.

00:59:45.120 --> 00:59:47.610
But you know, that is why.

00:59:47.610 --> 00:59:51.000
So some of this is people are doing better

00:59:51.000 --> 00:59:52.920
than their initial plans

00:59:52.920 --> 00:59:54.420
in getting through elective work

00:59:54.420 --> 00:59:55.980
and that is in part also reflected

00:59:55.980 --> 00:59:58.260
in the sort of improvement
in productivity.

00:59:58.260 --> 00:59:59.093
- [Wol] Thank you

01:00:00.173 --> 01:00:01.170
- Andrew, Richard?

01:00:01.170 --> 01:00:04.680
- So this feels really fragile, Julian,

01:00:04.680 --> 01:00:06.600
particularly in a number of settings

01:00:06.600 --> 01:00:09.780
I know are experiencing real difficulties

01:00:09.780 --> 01:00:11.133
in hitting the plan.

01:00:12.210 --> 01:00:14.963
To what extent are we
supporting those systems?

01:00:14.963 --> 01:00:18.330
I mean, I would urge
all the board's concern

01:00:18.330 --> 01:00:22.230
that, you know, they really,
really focus on the money.

01:00:22.230 --> 01:00:24.030
That there's also, you know,

01:00:24.030 --> 01:00:27.630
the issue of delivery against
four hours and the electives.

01:00:27.630 --> 01:00:30.240
It's a really challenging agenda.

01:00:30.240 --> 01:00:33.213
But ultimately, you know, we,

01:00:34.050 --> 01:00:36.060
if we haven't got the cash,

01:00:36.060 --> 01:00:37.800
we can't do a hell of a lot.

01:00:37.800 --> 01:00:41.010
So we are reliant on
boards to deliver the plans

01:00:41.010 --> 01:00:43.410
that they set off and agreed to

01:00:43.410 --> 01:00:45.390
at the beginning of this year.

01:00:45.390 --> 01:00:48.900
And it's their responsibility
to do their bit

01:00:48.900 --> 01:00:52.560
unless there are, you know,
significant mitigating factors.

01:00:52.560 --> 01:00:55.770
And it'd be just helpful
just to give a flavour

01:00:55.770 --> 01:00:58.350
of some of the support we are
giving those organisations

01:00:58.350 --> 01:01:00.120
that are really struggling.

01:01:00.120 --> 01:01:02.340
- And I know you've seen some
of this in some of the RSPs

01:01:02.340 --> 01:01:03.957
that you chair and so on, yeah.

01:01:03.957 --> 01:01:07.380
- And I think, Richard, some
boards got off the mark,

01:01:07.380 --> 01:01:09.990
they may have a really challenging agenda,

01:01:09.990 --> 01:01:11.490
but they've got off the mark

01:01:11.490 --> 01:01:13.560
at the beginning of the financial year

01:01:13.560 --> 01:01:16.830
and done so with a great
deal of discipline.

01:01:16.830 --> 01:01:18.870
I think in other settings that discipline

01:01:18.870 --> 01:01:21.750
hasn't manifested itself in the results

01:01:21.750 --> 01:01:23.670
that they signed or the, you know,

01:01:23.670 --> 01:01:26.160
the plans that they signed up to.

01:01:26.160 --> 01:01:27.510
So there's a real challenge now

01:01:27.510 --> 01:01:29.550
we're halfway through the year.

01:01:29.550 --> 01:01:30.383
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:01:30.383 --> 01:01:32.490
- And some providers have got

01:01:32.490 --> 01:01:35.820
really, really demanding targets

01:01:35.820 --> 01:01:37.800
to deliver by the year end.

01:01:37.800 --> 01:01:40.500
So it's pretty, pretty gritty start.

01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:41.570
- But the stake in all
of this is, you know,

01:01:41.570 --> 01:01:44.010
if you have to get a saving in 12 months

01:01:44.010 --> 01:01:46.260
and you've only then got six
months left to get the saving,

01:01:46.260 --> 01:01:48.120
you've got to get twice the rate.

01:01:48.120 --> 01:01:51.990
So it's very difficult.
- So is really gritty stuff,

01:01:51.990 --> 01:01:53.673
you know, we have identified,

01:01:55.230 --> 01:01:57.450
you know, we are working with, I forget,

01:01:57.450 --> 01:01:59.090
I should know the precise
number off the top of my head,

01:01:59.090 --> 01:02:04.020
but like 10 to 12 systems
where we have buddied up

01:02:04.020 --> 01:02:08.040
a very, you know, senior
NHS finance person

01:02:08.040 --> 01:02:09.570
with some consultancy support

01:02:09.570 --> 01:02:13.770
that we have asked them to source,

01:02:13.770 --> 01:02:15.630
to really look at their plans,

01:02:15.630 --> 01:02:16.463
actually look at the way, you know,

01:02:16.463 --> 01:02:18.330
have they really got
the mechanisms in place

01:02:18.330 --> 01:02:19.560
to deliver their plans

01:02:19.560 --> 01:02:21.930
and what further
opportunities might they have?

01:02:21.930 --> 01:02:24.390
I will say one of the things

01:02:24.390 --> 01:02:27.150
that they are consistently
identifying in those places,

01:02:27.150 --> 01:02:29.070
which are further off plan,

01:02:29.070 --> 01:02:31.350
is actually that the
kind of basic controls,

01:02:31.350 --> 01:02:33.570
although, and sometimes
in paper they're in place,

01:02:33.570 --> 01:02:35.780
they're not working effectively and up.

01:02:35.780 --> 01:02:39.990
So in part it is actually
working with them

01:02:39.990 --> 01:02:41.910
actually just to get some
of the basic controls,

01:02:41.910 --> 01:02:44.880
decision making about jobs and vacancies

01:02:44.880 --> 01:02:47.220
and, you know,

01:02:47.220 --> 01:02:49.950
the approval of business
cases and things of that ilk

01:02:49.950 --> 01:02:52.140
just to make sure when
the money is this tight,

01:02:52.140 --> 01:02:53.280
the disciplines,

01:02:53.280 --> 01:02:56.940
the basic disciplines
are really working well.

01:02:56.940 --> 01:03:01.230
Jackie and Andrew knew from supply chain,

01:03:01.230 --> 01:03:05.350
we are trying to identify where
they can support to go into

01:03:06.300 --> 01:03:08.370
providers and Trusts literally to look at

01:03:08.370 --> 01:03:10.194
the commercial spend

01:03:10.194 --> 01:03:14.400
and identify where we
know we can buy things

01:03:14.400 --> 01:03:18.000
through supply chain at a better price.

01:03:18.000 --> 01:03:21.570
You know, we're doing the
work on energy contracts,

01:03:21.570 --> 01:03:24.900
so we're trying to find
in the commercial space.

01:03:24.900 --> 01:03:26.340
We are

01:03:26.340 --> 01:03:27.900
working up actually through

01:03:27.900 --> 01:03:31.800
some of the improvement work programmes

01:03:31.800 --> 01:03:34.620
to identify like what
best practise looks like,

01:03:34.620 --> 01:03:36.310
frankly on rostering

01:03:37.320 --> 01:03:39.150
and deployment of staff

01:03:39.150 --> 01:03:42.960
and to try and source like expertise

01:03:42.960 --> 01:03:47.730
to gather people together
because some of this is

01:03:47.730 --> 01:03:51.210
we've also had quite turnover
of general management

01:03:51.210 --> 01:03:52.857
and how do we sort of give people

01:03:52.857 --> 01:03:56.280
some of the, you know,
just support across the NHS

01:03:56.280 --> 01:03:58.290
to sort of give people
some of the basic skills

01:03:58.290 --> 01:03:59.123
that they need?

01:03:59.123 --> 01:04:00.390
So that sort of gives you a flavour

01:04:00.390 --> 01:04:02.850
of like the short-term specifics

01:04:02.850 --> 01:04:04.530
where we can honestly go in

01:04:04.530 --> 01:04:08.460
and sort of identify new opportunities

01:04:08.460 --> 01:04:10.680
as well as how do we support

01:04:10.680 --> 01:04:11.513
some of the improvement work,

01:04:11.513 --> 01:04:14.820
which is actually what is
going to be necessary to make,

01:04:14.820 --> 01:04:18.090
you know, sustained
and sustainable change.

01:04:18.090 --> 01:04:21.210
- And failure to deliver
this year causes a problem

01:04:21.210 --> 01:04:23.733
for next year for these
organisations as well.

01:04:24.780 --> 01:04:26.340
- And you know, my
encouragement to boards is

01:04:26.340 --> 01:04:28.920
you really have to look now
at the kind of workforce

01:04:28.920 --> 01:04:33.090
as you go through
September, October, November

01:04:33.090 --> 01:04:36.060
because that is actually
where the growth last year was

01:04:36.060 --> 01:04:38.850
much larger than people had planned for.

01:04:38.850 --> 01:04:40.620
And I think this is the
moment where it's great,

01:04:40.620 --> 01:04:42.690
recruit substantive staff,

01:04:42.690 --> 01:04:43.980
that is the way we want it,

01:04:43.980 --> 01:04:47.910
but you have got to take out
the temporary workforce costs

01:04:47.910 --> 01:04:51.030
that were otherwise plugging the gaps

01:04:51.030 --> 01:04:54.090
and that requires some
management discipline.

01:04:54.090 --> 01:04:55.863
- Thank you, any other, Jane?

01:04:56.730 --> 01:04:58.950
- Just a couple points if I can.

01:04:58.950 --> 01:05:00.175
The Darzi report,

01:05:00.175 --> 01:05:03.000
which I agree, very, very
clear, very good report

01:05:03.000 --> 01:05:08.000
was also extremely clear about
the need to not raid capital,

01:05:08.430 --> 01:05:09.900
to keep capital budgets,

01:05:09.900 --> 01:05:12.000
to maintain them and
to invest them wisely.

01:05:12.000 --> 01:05:13.950
And obviously, you know, as a board

01:05:13.950 --> 01:05:16.680
we've expressed
particularly strong interest

01:05:16.680 --> 01:05:21.390
around investing in long-term tech and IT.

01:05:21.390 --> 01:05:23.220
Just wondered if you could expand a bit

01:05:23.220 --> 01:05:25.255
on where that sort of conversation is at

01:05:25.255 --> 01:05:29.097
'cause obviously lost a bit
of time in terms of, you know,

01:05:30.090 --> 01:05:32.730
the kind of capital plans
that were being discussed

01:05:32.730 --> 01:05:35.910
around the time of the
pre-election budget.

01:05:35.910 --> 01:05:37.410
So just wondered if there's anything

01:05:37.410 --> 01:05:38.790
that can be said on that,

01:05:38.790 --> 01:05:41.550
picking up Darzi's emphasis on it.

01:05:41.550 --> 01:05:44.610
And the second is just around elective.

01:05:44.610 --> 01:05:48.420
I mean Amanda mentioned
the Further Faster groups.

01:05:48.420 --> 01:05:52.110
I think it was obviously a lot of emphasis

01:05:52.110 --> 01:05:54.030
on that announcement recently.

01:05:54.030 --> 01:05:56.850
I just wonder as they get going

01:05:56.850 --> 01:05:59.640
and sort of, you know, are evaluated,

01:05:59.640 --> 01:06:03.090
will there be any attempt
to feed in the learnings

01:06:03.090 --> 01:06:06.150
from that process into
the wider elective piece

01:06:06.150 --> 01:06:07.650
to kind of, if you like,

01:06:07.650 --> 01:06:10.320
give that more focus and direction?

01:06:10.320 --> 01:06:11.310
Because obviously at the moment

01:06:11.310 --> 01:06:12.660
we've got this slight conundrum

01:06:12.660 --> 01:06:15.210
that there's lots of
fantastic activity going on,

01:06:15.210 --> 01:06:17.010
colleagues in NHS are
doing all this great work,

01:06:17.010 --> 01:06:18.840
but it's also creating a pressure.

01:06:18.840 --> 01:06:20.820
So I just wondered whether
there's any opportunity for that

01:06:20.820 --> 01:06:23.043
to become more focused over time?

01:06:24.615 --> 01:06:27.810
- Go to the first one,
Julian, on the capital.

01:06:27.810 --> 01:06:31.470
- Really clearly we continue
to try and prioritise

01:06:31.470 --> 01:06:33.570
capital investment.

01:06:33.570 --> 01:06:37.743
You know, last year with the
department and the government,

01:06:38.910 --> 01:06:40.170
you know, there was the need

01:06:40.170 --> 01:06:41.587
that where there were programmes

01:06:41.587 --> 01:06:45.120
that were underspending
as opposed to decisions

01:06:45.120 --> 01:06:47.340
to stop things that there was a, you know,

01:06:47.340 --> 01:06:51.900
there was a requirement to move
capital to revenue in order,

01:06:51.900 --> 01:06:54.930
I mean basically to keep the
sort of show on the road,

01:06:54.930 --> 01:06:56.160
the operation on the road.

01:06:56.160 --> 01:06:59.370
And sometimes you face these, you know,

01:06:59.370 --> 01:07:02.700
I mean it isn't actually our call on that,

01:07:02.700 --> 01:07:06.870
but you know, the sort of
almost necessary decision

01:07:06.870 --> 01:07:09.720
as to, you know, how do you
deal with the day-to-day?

01:07:09.720 --> 01:07:12.840
And no one wants to
move capital to revenue,

01:07:12.840 --> 01:07:14.370
but if you really have no choice,

01:07:14.370 --> 01:07:18.180
how do you keep serving patients today

01:07:18.180 --> 01:07:20.700
where that is an absolute necessity?

01:07:20.700 --> 01:07:22.563
So I think it's clear, you know,

01:07:24.690 --> 01:07:27.210
that is not where people want to end up,

01:07:27.210 --> 01:07:31.200
but under like a force
majeure circumstance

01:07:31.200 --> 01:07:32.760
that can become necessary.

01:07:32.760 --> 01:07:35.520
I mean, let's see what, how
we go through this year.

01:07:35.520 --> 01:07:36.480
- And I would just add,

01:07:36.480 --> 01:07:40.890
I think I referenced in
my opening sort of remarks

01:07:40.890 --> 01:07:43.530
about some key elements been working on,

01:07:43.530 --> 01:07:45.780
we at the NHS England board,

01:07:45.780 --> 01:07:47.910
so the NHS England team
have been working on

01:07:47.910 --> 01:07:50.730
and that clearly the Long-Term
Infrastructure Strategy

01:07:50.730 --> 01:07:53.580
and I would hope very much
it's clear they would feature

01:07:53.580 --> 01:07:55.530
as part of the 10-year health plan.

01:07:55.530 --> 01:07:57.450
And so let's see.

01:07:57.450 --> 01:08:00.210
Do you want to pick up on
the point about the lesson,

01:08:00.210 --> 01:08:02.130
you know, how we take lessons learned

01:08:02.130 --> 01:08:06.840
from the initiative in the
20 areas of deprivation

01:08:06.840 --> 01:08:09.780
where we're seeing people
out of work with ill health

01:08:09.780 --> 01:08:11.820
and concentrating effort there?

01:08:11.820 --> 01:08:13.740
- Yeah, no, I mean absolutely.

01:08:13.740 --> 01:08:16.110
So I think just worth
saying that Tim Briggs,

01:08:16.110 --> 01:08:17.550
who leads the GIRFT programme,

01:08:17.550 --> 01:08:21.390
has been an absolutely core
member of the elective team

01:08:21.390 --> 01:08:24.660
within NHS England for some
considerable period of time.

01:08:24.660 --> 01:08:27.180
So he works really
closely with Jim Mackey,

01:08:27.180 --> 01:08:29.280
who is the SRO for that programme

01:08:29.280 --> 01:08:33.090
and has run a series of Further Faster,

01:08:33.090 --> 01:08:37.290
I think three now cohorts
of that programme have run.

01:08:37.290 --> 01:08:38.583
So in a sense it's the other way around,

01:08:38.583 --> 01:08:40.590
that actually what's happened is

01:08:40.590 --> 01:08:42.696
what he has been able
to do with colleagues.

01:08:42.696 --> 01:08:43.650
- That's right
- Is learn from

01:08:43.650 --> 01:08:45.570
what has happened up till now

01:08:45.570 --> 01:08:49.650
to be able to then shape a
programme that will focus,

01:08:49.650 --> 01:08:52.350
as you say, on the sort
of Further Faster 20.

01:08:52.350 --> 01:08:54.720
So I think that has been
a real learning experience

01:08:54.720 --> 01:08:56.700
over the last few years,

01:08:56.700 --> 01:09:00.540
but really understanding
what that combination,

01:09:00.540 --> 01:09:01.373
as I said earlier,

01:09:01.373 --> 01:09:03.390
of the clinically led, data-driven,

01:09:03.390 --> 01:09:06.270
improvement-oriented intervention

01:09:06.270 --> 01:09:10.860
can help organisations on
the ground, as it were,

01:09:10.860 --> 01:09:15.150
actually do very practically to improve

01:09:15.150 --> 01:09:16.860
their productivity, their efficiency,

01:09:16.860 --> 01:09:18.690
their outcomes, their quality,

01:09:18.690 --> 01:09:20.510
their patient experience, you know,

01:09:20.510 --> 01:09:23.460
so things that really matter
to patients and to staff

01:09:23.460 --> 01:09:26.070
as well as some of
those headline outcomes.

01:09:26.070 --> 01:09:29.940
So I think we really
welcome now, you know,

01:09:29.940 --> 01:09:32.760
that renewed focus if you
like on using improvement

01:09:32.760 --> 01:09:36.420
as a key tool to drive both
efficiency and quality.

01:09:36.420 --> 01:09:39.750
So with Sarah-Jane,
with Emily, with others,

01:09:39.750 --> 01:09:41.250
there's a lot of work going on

01:09:41.250 --> 01:09:43.920
under that NHS IMPACT
umbrella at the moment

01:09:43.920 --> 01:09:46.230
to make sure that we really are using

01:09:46.230 --> 01:09:48.470
the best of all the bits
that we've got at the moment

01:09:48.470 --> 01:09:51.960
at scale as we think about the
sort of challenge of delivery

01:09:51.960 --> 01:09:55.440
going forward.
- Thank you, Vin.

01:09:55.440 --> 01:09:57.470
- Yeah, two great
points, Chair, thank you.

01:09:57.470 --> 01:09:59.340
So the first thing is in
addition to the things

01:09:59.340 --> 01:10:00.510
we've mentioned,

01:10:00.510 --> 01:10:03.000
I think under the programme
we've made a big investment

01:10:03.000 --> 01:10:05.850
in data and improving our data flow.

01:10:05.850 --> 01:10:07.770
So there are new data compartments

01:10:07.770 --> 01:10:08.850
on the model health system

01:10:08.850 --> 01:10:12.150
that will enable Trusts and
ICBs to compare themselves

01:10:12.150 --> 01:10:14.010
and benchmark themselves.

01:10:14.010 --> 01:10:16.170
In the private board earlier today,

01:10:16.170 --> 01:10:19.380
we saw a great example of how
the Federated Data Platform

01:10:19.380 --> 01:10:22.020
can be used both to improve productivity,

01:10:22.020 --> 01:10:24.780
make best use of the
staffing that you have got.

01:10:24.780 --> 01:10:27.303
And then just reflecting
on my previous background

01:10:27.303 --> 01:10:28.710
as a medical director,

01:10:28.710 --> 01:10:30.470
and I think all of us
here have been on board,

01:10:30.470 --> 01:10:32.610
so this is, I just want to
reemphasize with Julian,

01:10:32.610 --> 01:10:35.550
this is not just a finance responsibility.

01:10:35.550 --> 01:10:38.730
When you work in a really
effective executive team,

01:10:38.730 --> 01:10:40.740
the medical director, the
nurse director and others

01:10:40.740 --> 01:10:42.960
take as much responsibility

01:10:42.960 --> 01:10:44.850
not just for delivering the plan,

01:10:44.850 --> 01:10:47.943
but also making sure that
quality is maintained as well.

01:10:48.780 --> 01:10:50.940
- Vin, thank you, Steve,
last point on this.

01:10:50.940 --> 01:10:52.677
- I think that's a really important point

01:10:52.677 --> 01:10:53.820
and in the last,

01:10:53.820 --> 01:10:55.380
over the last few months I've done

01:10:55.380 --> 01:10:57.750
my currently twice-yearly meetings

01:10:57.750 --> 01:10:59.910
with all the medical
directors around the country

01:10:59.910 --> 01:11:02.010
and the, and we have

01:11:02.010 --> 01:11:05.040
almost all of those had a
discussion around productivity.

01:11:05.040 --> 01:11:06.717
We brought the productivity team

01:11:06.717 --> 01:11:10.410
and the point that I've
consistently made is that,

01:11:10.410 --> 01:11:12.120
particularly now, of course always,

01:11:12.120 --> 01:11:12.953
but particularly now,

01:11:12.953 --> 01:11:14.760
it's the job of medical,
nursing directors,

01:11:14.760 --> 01:11:18.840
other clinical leaders to support
their financial colleagues

01:11:18.840 --> 01:11:21.990
to ensure that balance between ensuring

01:11:21.990 --> 01:11:25.230
that quality is maintained and,

01:11:25.230 --> 01:11:29.690
but also recognising the
financial challenge that we have

01:11:29.690 --> 01:11:31.050
is the right balance.

01:11:31.050 --> 01:11:33.060
And actually when I
meet finance directors,

01:11:33.060 --> 01:11:34.860
it's the same message
the other way around,

01:11:34.860 --> 01:11:37.470
that this is the time
that finance directors

01:11:37.470 --> 01:11:40.263
more than any time have
to have that, you know,

01:11:40.263 --> 01:11:42.450
that quality, you know,

01:11:42.450 --> 01:11:45.060
thing on their shoulder
tapping and saying,

01:11:45.060 --> 01:11:46.315
we paid attention to this.

01:11:46.315 --> 01:11:48.480
So this is not about one group or another.

01:11:48.480 --> 01:11:51.240
This is around, you know, both groups

01:11:51.240 --> 01:11:53.460
and of course, you know,
really good boards,

01:11:53.460 --> 01:11:56.280
whether it's ICBs or provider trusts

01:11:56.280 --> 01:11:59.490
have always and will continue
to get that balance right

01:11:59.490 --> 01:12:01.620
and have it at the
forefront of their minds.

01:12:01.620 --> 01:12:02.453
- Thank you.

01:12:02.453 --> 01:12:04.020
I'm going to move us
on if that's all right.

01:12:04.020 --> 01:12:07.083
We've got two substantive
items still to cover.

01:12:08.580 --> 01:12:11.370
The first, actually
Amanda, it's you I think,

01:12:11.370 --> 01:12:14.760
and then with Kevin Lavery and Andy Knox,

01:12:14.760 --> 01:12:17.010
but on integrated health and care.

01:12:17.010 --> 01:12:18.570
Do you want to introduce it, Amanda?

01:12:18.570 --> 01:12:19.403
- Thank you.

01:12:19.403 --> 01:12:20.236
So

01:12:21.570 --> 01:12:24.750
the strategy for the NHS over recent years

01:12:24.750 --> 01:12:26.850
from the Longterm Plan and then built upon

01:12:26.850 --> 01:12:27.683
by the Fuller Stocktake

01:12:27.683 --> 01:12:31.560
and now more recently
emphasised in the Darzi review

01:12:31.560 --> 01:12:36.560
is about boosting care out
of hospital for patients,

01:12:37.890 --> 01:12:40.350
focusing on better care
for long-term conditions

01:12:40.350 --> 01:12:45.350
and improving the way in
which services are integrated

01:12:45.390 --> 01:12:48.030
so that care is more seamless for patients

01:12:48.030 --> 01:12:50.190
and therefore outcomes are improved.

01:12:50.190 --> 01:12:53.070
We've now also got commitment
from the government

01:12:53.070 --> 01:12:56.280
to three strategic shifts for the NHS,

01:12:56.280 --> 01:13:01.050
which are hospital to primary
care and community services,

01:13:01.050 --> 01:13:02.580
analogue to digital

01:13:02.580 --> 01:13:05.550
and from treatment to prevention.

01:13:05.550 --> 01:13:06.910
And all of this

01:13:07.890 --> 01:13:10.380
approach combined

01:13:10.380 --> 01:13:11.213
to be

01:13:12.690 --> 01:13:14.820
manifested almost through the development

01:13:14.820 --> 01:13:16.320
of Neighbourhood Health Services

01:13:16.320 --> 01:13:21.320
where people can be optimally
treated closer to home,

01:13:21.780 --> 01:13:25.290
where they can get as much
of their care as is possible

01:13:25.290 --> 01:13:27.540
without being admitted to hospital.

01:13:27.540 --> 01:13:29.190
And where there's a real focus on

01:13:29.190 --> 01:13:32.400
not only prevention of
development of disease,

01:13:32.400 --> 01:13:36.780
but prevention of exacerbation
of disease and frailty

01:13:36.780 --> 01:13:38.580
so that unavoidable,

01:13:38.580 --> 01:13:41.220
so that avoidable treatments
and hospital admissions

01:13:41.220 --> 01:13:43.020
can be put in place.

01:13:43.020 --> 01:13:45.180
This is particularly
important in populations

01:13:45.180 --> 01:13:47.670
with the greatest health inequalities

01:13:47.670 --> 01:13:50.280
and the greatest health challenges

01:13:50.280 --> 01:13:51.927
resulting in the poorest outcomes.

01:13:51.927 --> 01:13:54.630
And so I think there's a real opportunity

01:13:54.630 --> 01:13:57.510
while we're sat here in possibly the place

01:13:57.510 --> 01:14:00.900
with the greatest health
challenge in the country

01:14:00.900 --> 01:14:02.820
to listen to some of our local leaders

01:14:02.820 --> 01:14:06.150
about the things they
are doing to help improve

01:14:06.150 --> 01:14:08.100
the health of their population

01:14:08.100 --> 01:14:11.007
through looking at how
they integrate services.

01:14:11.007 --> 01:14:12.930
And so we've got Kevin Lavery,

01:14:12.930 --> 01:14:16.380
Chief Exec of the ICB in
Lancashire and South Cumbria

01:14:16.380 --> 01:14:19.860
and Andy Knox, who is an
Associate Medical Director

01:14:19.860 --> 01:14:21.810
but also has been a GP in Lancashire

01:14:21.810 --> 01:14:24.120
for quite a long time now.

01:14:24.120 --> 01:14:24.953
- 12 years now,

01:14:24.953 --> 01:14:26.760
14 years in Manchester before.

01:14:26.760 --> 01:14:30.330
- So I'll stop talking, I'll
hand over to Kevin and Andy.

01:14:30.330 --> 01:14:32.820
- Great, well colleagues,
welcome to Blackpool.

01:14:32.820 --> 01:14:35.040
It's really great to have you with us.

01:14:35.040 --> 01:14:37.680
Not just famous for when
"Strictly" comes to town also,

01:14:37.680 --> 01:14:41.160
but if you've not taken the
opportunity to ride the Big One,

01:14:41.160 --> 01:14:42.570
you need to do so before you go.

01:14:42.570 --> 01:14:44.910
It's without a doubt my
favourite rollercoaster.

01:14:44.910 --> 01:14:45.743
So

01:14:47.340 --> 01:14:50.463
listen, Blackpool, as
Amanda has so kindly,

01:14:51.570 --> 01:14:53.250
I mean she knows this town so well,

01:14:53.250 --> 01:14:55.440
she has served this town so incredibly

01:14:55.440 --> 01:14:57.270
and the whole of Lancashire
and South Cumbria

01:14:57.270 --> 01:14:58.440
for a long time.

01:14:58.440 --> 01:14:59.673
And Amanda, thank you.

01:15:01.620 --> 01:15:04.320
There are some really important things

01:15:04.320 --> 01:15:07.050
to take hold of Blackpool
as you spend some time here.

01:15:07.050 --> 01:15:09.540
And we're excited to show
you some of the great work

01:15:09.540 --> 01:15:11.973
that's going on across Blackpool tomorrow.

01:15:12.840 --> 01:15:15.330
This town has the lowest life expectancy

01:15:15.330 --> 01:15:16.770
of any town in England.

01:15:16.770 --> 01:15:20.040
It has the lowest healthy life expectancy

01:15:20.040 --> 01:15:21.390
of any town in England.

01:15:21.390 --> 01:15:24.390
Men here are in their early 50s,

01:15:24.390 --> 01:15:28.440
53 before they're getting
significant ill health problems

01:15:28.440 --> 01:15:29.910
and women only 54.

01:15:29.910 --> 01:15:33.390
That is significantly worse
than other parts of England.

01:15:33.390 --> 01:15:36.930
People hit early, hit significant frailty

01:15:36.930 --> 01:15:38.640
in their early 50s here

01:15:38.640 --> 01:15:41.670
with high burden of chronic
disease across the town.

01:15:41.670 --> 01:15:44.070
20% of the population here smoke.

01:15:44.070 --> 01:15:48.330
22% of the adult population
say they are depressed.

01:15:48.330 --> 01:15:52.440
18% compared to 13%
nationally have hypertension.

01:15:52.440 --> 01:15:54.480
9% have diabetes,

01:15:54.480 --> 01:15:59.070
75% of our adult population is overweight.

01:15:59.070 --> 01:16:02.043
That's 82,000 adults in the town.

01:16:03.390 --> 01:16:07.020
And then when you think
about what the impact is

01:16:07.020 --> 01:16:08.160
on children,

01:16:08.160 --> 01:16:10.560
we have three times the national average

01:16:10.560 --> 01:16:13.530
number of children in care in Blackpool,

01:16:13.530 --> 01:16:16.170
we have the lowest educational attainment

01:16:16.170 --> 01:16:18.240
of any children in the country

01:16:18.240 --> 01:16:20.700
and very, very high
population of under fives.

01:16:20.700 --> 01:16:22.920
So you can, what I just wanted to say,

01:16:22.920 --> 01:16:25.290
there's a stark picture here, right?

01:16:25.290 --> 01:16:27.000
And yet it's a wonderful town

01:16:27.000 --> 01:16:28.200
full of huge potential

01:16:28.200 --> 01:16:30.950
and actually amazing things
happening at the same time.

01:16:31.800 --> 01:16:34.140
We've got a really high number

01:16:34.140 --> 01:16:36.030
of asylum seekers in the town.

01:16:36.030 --> 01:16:40.140
One of the groups tomorrow
will see the work of FCMS here,

01:16:40.140 --> 01:16:42.600
but you won't be going to
the asylum seeker work.

01:16:42.600 --> 01:16:45.720
But if you took a stroll
down to the Metropol Hotel,

01:16:45.720 --> 01:16:48.870
you'll see hundreds and
hundreds of asylum seekers

01:16:48.870 --> 01:16:51.240
all crammed into a hotel together

01:16:51.240 --> 01:16:53.940
where actually currently
we have 18 pregnant women

01:16:53.940 --> 01:16:58.470
and a host of unbelievable,
complex tropical disease

01:16:58.470 --> 01:17:01.353
and significant mental trauma.

01:17:03.030 --> 01:17:05.620
So there's complexity to deal with here

01:17:06.630 --> 01:17:07.463
and

01:17:08.430 --> 01:17:09.263
although

01:17:10.260 --> 01:17:12.510
we've got very, very
high levels of poverty,

01:17:12.510 --> 01:17:14.100
in fact in some wards,

01:17:14.100 --> 01:17:16.440
70 to 80% of the population

01:17:16.440 --> 01:17:19.350
live in the poorest 20% of the population

01:17:19.350 --> 01:17:21.870
in terms of index and
multiple deprivation.

01:17:21.870 --> 01:17:25.020
And when you look at the
housing in Blackpool,

01:17:25.020 --> 01:17:26.400
I think when you walk the streets,

01:17:26.400 --> 01:17:28.170
some of it could potentially shock you.

01:17:28.170 --> 01:17:32.610
75% of our private rented sector housing

01:17:32.610 --> 01:17:34.950
is damp and/or mouldy,

01:17:34.950 --> 01:17:37.620
which has a massive impact
on respiratory disease

01:17:37.620 --> 01:17:38.880
in the town as well,

01:17:38.880 --> 01:17:42.570
with low earnings and only
66.5% of our residents

01:17:42.570 --> 01:17:45.690
being economically active
compared to 78% nationally.

01:17:45.690 --> 01:17:47.580
So some big challenges for us

01:17:47.580 --> 01:17:50.790
and the truth is that
actually our model of care

01:17:50.790 --> 01:17:52.170
therefore is unsustainable.

01:17:52.170 --> 01:17:54.780
If you multiply that across
Lancashire and South Cumbria,

01:17:54.780 --> 01:17:56.460
Blackpool is unique

01:17:56.460 --> 01:17:59.160
but actually not far
behind it are places like

01:17:59.160 --> 01:18:00.630
East Lancashire, Hyndburn,

01:18:00.630 --> 01:18:03.390
Burnley, Blackburn, Barrow-in-Furness.

01:18:03.390 --> 01:18:06.480
We've got some big things
to contend with together

01:18:06.480 --> 01:18:07.313
and if we carry on as we are,

01:18:07.313 --> 01:18:12.313
by 2038, we are going to
need 60% more hospital beds

01:18:12.407 --> 01:18:14.220
than we've got now.

01:18:14.220 --> 01:18:17.340
And there's going to be a 15%
rise in long-term conditions

01:18:17.340 --> 01:18:18.420
and chronic ill health.

01:18:18.420 --> 01:18:22.020
That is financially an
impossible conundrum.

01:18:22.020 --> 01:18:24.720
So we know there's got to
be a radical major shift

01:18:24.720 --> 01:18:26.520
and we really welcome the Darzi report

01:18:26.520 --> 01:18:29.010
as a result of many of the
things that he's saying.

01:18:29.010 --> 01:18:30.450
So we're trying to take an approach here

01:18:30.450 --> 01:18:32.087
in Lancashire and South
Cumbria to improve the health

01:18:32.087 --> 01:18:33.510
of our population

01:18:33.510 --> 01:18:36.120
through the tackling of health inequity.

01:18:36.120 --> 01:18:38.280
And so here are some of the
things that we are on with

01:18:38.280 --> 01:18:40.860
in terms of obviously integrating care

01:18:40.860 --> 01:18:41.940
as much as we possibly can

01:18:41.940 --> 01:18:43.980
and shifting towards prevention,

01:18:43.980 --> 01:18:46.680
really seeing the power and
potential of primary care

01:18:46.680 --> 01:18:48.690
in partnership with our
public health colleagues

01:18:48.690 --> 01:18:50.850
and our community voluntary faith sector.

01:18:50.850 --> 01:18:52.110
So over the last couple of years

01:18:52.110 --> 01:18:56.310
we've taken 48 clinical
leaders in primary care

01:18:56.310 --> 01:18:58.650
through a population
health leadership academy

01:18:58.650 --> 01:19:03.450
and they, we now have 48 health
inequity clinical leaders

01:19:03.450 --> 01:19:05.220
in each one of our PCNs.

01:19:05.220 --> 01:19:08.160
I was with many of them this
afternoon across Blackpool

01:19:08.160 --> 01:19:10.440
looking at the incredible
work that they're doing

01:19:10.440 --> 01:19:12.420
around detecting and protecting people

01:19:12.420 --> 01:19:14.850
from the effects of hypertension.

01:19:14.850 --> 01:19:16.680
Last year we took 48 senior leaders

01:19:16.680 --> 01:19:17.513
through our Trusts

01:19:17.513 --> 01:19:19.740
and this year we've got another 48 leaders

01:19:19.740 --> 01:19:21.120
from primary and secondary care

01:19:21.120 --> 01:19:22.050
going through that programme

01:19:22.050 --> 01:19:25.140
and 18 members of our exec
teams and board teams,

01:19:25.140 --> 01:19:27.510
which is fantastic because
it's beginning to change

01:19:27.510 --> 01:19:29.040
our focus and our lenses

01:19:29.040 --> 01:19:30.840
towards a health equity approach,

01:19:30.840 --> 01:19:31.920
which makes a big difference

01:19:31.920 --> 01:19:33.990
when you actually look
at the data of the people

01:19:33.990 --> 01:19:35.610
who's flowing into our Trusts

01:19:35.610 --> 01:19:37.380
and where our big cost lies,

01:19:37.380 --> 01:19:38.880
it actually comes from our communities

01:19:38.880 --> 01:19:40.200
who struggle most with their health.

01:19:40.200 --> 01:19:43.470
So we need to give better
focus and better integration

01:19:43.470 --> 01:19:45.900
into those neighbourhoods
and those communities.

01:19:45.900 --> 01:19:47.790
And you're going to see some
of this in action tomorrow,

01:19:47.790 --> 01:19:50.580
which is going to be really
exciting for us to show you.

01:19:50.580 --> 01:19:52.500
And you're going to see the power

01:19:52.500 --> 01:19:54.210
that primary care really has

01:19:54.210 --> 01:19:56.400
when it works in an integrated way

01:19:56.400 --> 01:19:59.700
with partners in our community
to really affect change.

01:19:59.700 --> 01:20:01.560
So in the last year for example,

01:20:01.560 --> 01:20:03.360
we've here in Blackpool,

01:20:03.360 --> 01:20:05.520
we've detected 5,000,

01:20:05.520 --> 01:20:08.190
sorry, we've detected 2,000 new people

01:20:08.190 --> 01:20:10.110
in terms of undetected hypertension

01:20:10.110 --> 01:20:12.930
and treated 5,000 new people to target.

01:20:12.930 --> 01:20:14.550
Over the whole of Lancashire
and South Cumbria,

01:20:14.550 --> 01:20:17.610
we've gone from 59% of
people treated to target

01:20:17.610 --> 01:20:20.070
to 72% just in one year.

01:20:20.070 --> 01:20:24.180
We've seen a 50% reduction
in our CAMHS waiting lists

01:20:24.180 --> 01:20:27.030
in places like Fleetwood
through innovative working

01:20:27.030 --> 01:20:29.460
with communities in a
radically different way.

01:20:29.460 --> 01:20:32.760
We've seen a 50% decrease
in ED attendances

01:20:32.760 --> 01:20:35.340
in East Lancashire for
our frail and elderly

01:20:35.340 --> 01:20:38.850
through the Enhancing
Better Care programme

01:20:38.850 --> 01:20:41.160
and taking the improvement approach.

01:20:41.160 --> 01:20:44.160
And we've seen a much,
much greater improvement

01:20:44.160 --> 01:20:46.980
in terms of early cancer
screening and diagnosis

01:20:46.980 --> 01:20:49.980
in our most disadvantaged communities.

01:20:49.980 --> 01:20:51.000
And this year we're focusing,

01:20:51.000 --> 01:20:52.290
particularly going into the winter,

01:20:52.290 --> 01:20:55.260
on our mental health crisis
and our respiratory crisis

01:20:55.260 --> 01:20:57.510
that we have in these communities.

01:20:57.510 --> 01:20:59.070
And later this month we will launch

01:20:59.070 --> 01:21:01.380
our system-wide Improvement Hub

01:21:01.380 --> 01:21:03.420
in conjunction with the
wonderful David Fillingham

01:21:03.420 --> 01:21:04.810
and Ailsa Brotherton

01:21:05.700 --> 01:21:07.200
as we try and recognise

01:21:07.200 --> 01:21:09.210
that it is only through a system approach

01:21:09.210 --> 01:21:10.547
and a left shift towards
improving the health

01:21:10.547 --> 01:21:12.000
of our population

01:21:12.000 --> 01:21:13.800
through the tackling of health inequity

01:21:13.800 --> 01:21:15.480
that we're going to deal with
some of the major problems

01:21:15.480 --> 01:21:16.410
that we're facing.

01:21:16.410 --> 01:21:18.330
But innovation is alive here,

01:21:18.330 --> 01:21:21.090
enthusiasm and gusto is alive here

01:21:21.090 --> 01:21:23.790
and we're beginning to work
in some really good, new ways

01:21:23.790 --> 01:21:25.574
in our communities.

01:21:25.574 --> 01:21:26.653
- Thanks, Andy.

01:21:26.653 --> 01:21:30.810
And thanks for the invitation
to attend this board.

01:21:30.810 --> 01:21:33.390
I think what Andy's been describing

01:21:33.390 --> 01:21:34.410
is a predicament, isn't it?

01:21:34.410 --> 01:21:36.397
It reminds me of Charles Dickens,

01:21:36.397 --> 01:21:37.500
"A Tale of two Cities",

01:21:37.500 --> 01:21:41.580
it was the best of times,
it was the worst of times.

01:21:41.580 --> 01:21:44.790
Blackpool's got some of
the really most difficult

01:21:44.790 --> 01:21:46.200
health challenges.

01:21:46.200 --> 01:21:47.280
But at the same time,

01:21:47.280 --> 01:21:49.500
there are some brilliant
opportunities here

01:21:49.500 --> 01:21:52.050
and some great innovations.

01:21:52.050 --> 01:21:55.200
And I guess the $64,000 question is

01:21:55.200 --> 01:21:57.360
what do we do about that?

01:21:57.360 --> 01:21:59.790
Understanding some of those
challenges in Blackpool

01:21:59.790 --> 01:22:03.810
and the wider Lancashire
and South Cumbria footprint,

01:22:03.810 --> 01:22:06.720
they're long term and they are systemic

01:22:06.720 --> 01:22:08.970
and many of them are system problems.

01:22:08.970 --> 01:22:11.910
Let me illustrate that with one example.

01:22:11.910 --> 01:22:13.860
If you look at the acute system,

01:22:13.860 --> 01:22:16.620
we've got eight medium sized hospitals,

01:22:16.620 --> 01:22:18.060
eight elective care centres,

01:22:18.060 --> 01:22:20.550
six accident and emergency centres,

01:22:20.550 --> 01:22:23.190
five sets of support services,

01:22:23.190 --> 01:22:24.327
mostly on old technology

01:22:24.327 --> 01:22:27.720
and in some cases on no technology.

01:22:27.720 --> 01:22:29.190
We were spending,

01:22:29.190 --> 01:22:32.460
Julian was talking about
agency staff earlier,

01:22:32.460 --> 01:22:35.580
300 million pounds a
year on temporary staff

01:22:35.580 --> 01:22:37.470
at premium rates.

01:22:37.470 --> 01:22:40.710
We've got big pressures in
urgent and emergency care.

01:22:40.710 --> 01:22:43.923
The cost of corridor
care alone, 84 million.

01:22:45.120 --> 01:22:50.070
And with that, we know it's
a relatively small number

01:22:50.070 --> 01:22:52.110
of people in deprived communities

01:22:52.110 --> 01:22:55.170
and frail elderly who are filling up

01:22:55.170 --> 01:22:59.430
accident emergency centres
and being repeat customers.

01:22:59.430 --> 01:23:01.410
They're all problems,
they're all challenges,

01:23:01.410 --> 01:23:04.830
but actually they're
also all opportunities.

01:23:04.830 --> 01:23:07.083
So we have a vision and plan

01:23:07.083 --> 01:23:10.530
that is trying to look at those challenges

01:23:10.530 --> 01:23:14.130
and convert those challenges
into opportunities.

01:23:14.130 --> 01:23:17.340
So it's that uplifting, positive theme.

01:23:17.340 --> 01:23:19.800
The recovery side of it is addressing

01:23:19.800 --> 01:23:22.260
exactly what Julian was covering earlier.

01:23:22.260 --> 01:23:24.420
All of the in-year issues,

01:23:24.420 --> 01:23:27.420
looking at vacancies, looking at costs,

01:23:27.420 --> 01:23:30.120
looking at discretionary spend.

01:23:30.120 --> 01:23:32.160
And then you've got the longer term reform

01:23:32.160 --> 01:23:33.480
around transformation,

01:23:33.480 --> 01:23:36.330
very much aligned to Lord Darzi's report

01:23:36.330 --> 01:23:39.390
and the early announcements
from the Secretary of State.

01:23:39.390 --> 01:23:42.180
There are three simple
elements to that plan.

01:23:42.180 --> 01:23:46.410
Our back office, so
we're now in the process

01:23:46.410 --> 01:23:50.460
of transferring close on 4,000 staff

01:23:50.460 --> 01:23:53.490
from five Trusts into one Trust

01:23:53.490 --> 01:23:57.420
to provide shared services,
support services back

01:23:57.420 --> 01:23:58.920
to the system.

01:23:58.920 --> 01:24:00.990
We reduced a collaborative,

01:24:00.990 --> 01:24:05.670
single collaborative bank for
Lancashire and South Cumbria

01:24:05.670 --> 01:24:06.840
in December last year,

01:24:06.840 --> 01:24:09.330
which is starting to bring costs down.

01:24:09.330 --> 01:24:13.380
These are real examples of change.

01:24:13.380 --> 01:24:15.510
Acute reconfiguration, we've just had

01:24:15.510 --> 01:24:17.880
some external consultants

01:24:17.880 --> 01:24:22.050
working with our acute
colleagues and our commissioners

01:24:22.050 --> 01:24:25.930
to effectively move to a networked

01:24:27.150 --> 01:24:28.680
acute system,

01:24:28.680 --> 01:24:32.400
which is based on quite a
lot of detailed benchmarking

01:24:32.400 --> 01:24:34.140
around individual specialties

01:24:34.140 --> 01:24:36.900
to look at how they're
sitting in terms of quality,

01:24:36.900 --> 01:24:39.690
patient safety and value for money.

01:24:39.690 --> 01:24:42.150
Very strong correlation between the three,

01:24:42.150 --> 01:24:43.440
interestingly enough,

01:24:43.440 --> 01:24:46.530
but huge variations across our network.

01:24:46.530 --> 01:24:49.560
And what we're trying to
do is get the best teams

01:24:49.560 --> 01:24:52.410
to work with the poorest performing teams

01:24:52.410 --> 01:24:54.690
within our system on that basis

01:24:54.690 --> 01:24:57.480
rather than simply go
for big group structure,

01:24:57.480 --> 01:24:59.550
big is beautiful approach.

01:24:59.550 --> 01:25:02.280
And then finally, and most importantly,

01:25:02.280 --> 01:25:04.893
primary care and community care,

01:25:07.050 --> 01:25:08.820
probably two big players here.

01:25:08.820 --> 01:25:11.130
One, and you've seen some of this Richard

01:25:11.130 --> 01:25:13.260
with your visit yesterday

01:25:13.260 --> 01:25:16.140
about bringing long-term
condition management

01:25:16.140 --> 01:25:19.140
out of acutes into community settings.

01:25:19.140 --> 01:25:22.050
But really getting on top of that UEC,

01:25:22.050 --> 01:25:25.380
that admissions avoidance area,

01:25:25.380 --> 01:25:28.230
using data to understand

01:25:28.230 --> 01:25:31.107
which people in deprived communities

01:25:31.107 --> 01:25:33.660
and in the acute elderly group

01:25:33.660 --> 01:25:36.060
are at most risk of being admitted.

01:25:36.060 --> 01:25:37.140
Making sure that we're,

01:25:37.140 --> 01:25:40.140
that they're being seen by GPs,

01:25:40.140 --> 01:25:43.080
there are proper care plans in place

01:25:43.080 --> 01:25:46.950
and there are appropriate
services at the local level

01:25:46.950 --> 01:25:49.440
to make sure that we keep them at home.

01:25:49.440 --> 01:25:51.810
The big target there needs to be

01:25:51.810 --> 01:25:54.090
something of the order of 30% reduction

01:25:54.090 --> 01:25:56.430
over a period of time in that space.

01:25:56.430 --> 01:25:58.530
But that's the big opportunity.

01:25:58.530 --> 01:26:03.180
I think there are some
areas where NHSE can help us

01:26:03.180 --> 01:26:06.300
do all of that faster,
better, smarter, cheaper.

01:26:06.300 --> 01:26:08.010
And we will be meeting Julian

01:26:08.010 --> 01:26:10.770
to look at some of those in due course,

01:26:10.770 --> 01:26:14.850
but I'd like to leave
you with one thought.

01:26:14.850 --> 01:26:19.200
We want to be famous as a system for,

01:26:19.200 --> 01:26:21.750
to be a place where
problems go to get solved

01:26:21.750 --> 01:26:25.050
rather than the place where
problems go to get really big.

01:26:25.050 --> 01:26:25.893
So thank you.

01:26:28.110 --> 01:26:29.220
- So thank you very much.

01:26:29.220 --> 01:26:30.053
I mean, I think

01:26:32.550 --> 01:26:35.340
really rather a, I mean
you presented very clearly

01:26:35.340 --> 01:26:39.810
and cogently a extremely
challenging picture,

01:26:39.810 --> 01:26:41.820
but also some of the steps forward

01:26:41.820 --> 01:26:43.770
and the avenues you are looking at

01:26:43.770 --> 01:26:47.910
that can help to mitigate
and turn this around.

01:26:47.910 --> 01:26:50.430
But I'm just going to open
up to comments and questions

01:26:50.430 --> 01:26:51.263
from colleagues.

01:26:51.263 --> 01:26:52.800
I can see a flood of hands going up,

01:26:52.800 --> 01:26:54.480
so I'm going to, I'll,

01:26:54.480 --> 01:26:58.170
we tend to do this, Kevin and
Andy, by batching questions,

01:26:58.170 --> 01:27:00.330
otherwise it's a bit
like watching Wimbledon

01:27:00.330 --> 01:27:01.350
or something with,

01:27:01.350 --> 01:27:03.720
so we batch it up and then you can choose

01:27:03.720 --> 01:27:06.240
which questions you answer, really.

01:27:06.240 --> 01:27:07.320
So we'll just work out.

01:27:07.320 --> 01:27:09.960
So Mark, let's go Mark and then Mike, we,

01:27:09.960 --> 01:27:10.980
I think your hand was up, Mike.

01:27:10.980 --> 01:27:12.840
So we go Mark and Mike,

01:27:12.840 --> 01:27:13.890
Steve, Wol,

01:27:13.890 --> 01:27:15.540
and then we will break for you to answer

01:27:15.540 --> 01:27:17.580
and we'll work this way around.

01:27:17.580 --> 01:27:19.560
- And this may not be a
question you can answer

01:27:19.560 --> 01:27:23.070
but an observation and Kevin,
Andy, thank you for that.

01:27:23.070 --> 01:27:27.393
And it's quite a sobering
picture of what you face.

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:31.620
There's one thing you said which was

01:27:31.620 --> 01:27:33.160
you're trying to fix this

01:27:34.290 --> 01:27:36.240
in an integrated way

01:27:36.240 --> 01:27:38.670
where we are all completely
bought into the vision

01:27:38.670 --> 01:27:42.630
of the 10-year plan and the
observations of Darzi report,

01:27:42.630 --> 01:27:45.753
but with no or little technology.

01:27:46.920 --> 01:27:49.320
And to actually address
what you're trying to do

01:27:50.310 --> 01:27:53.070
is going to need a technological solution.

01:27:53.070 --> 01:27:55.470
And one of the things that's
been bubbling around, Chair,

01:27:55.470 --> 01:27:59.010
recently is although the
10-year plan is underway

01:27:59.010 --> 01:28:01.800
and it's going to take
18 months to develop,

01:28:01.800 --> 01:28:04.383
there are some things
we are going to need,

01:28:06.090 --> 01:28:09.150
probably four things, a
single patient record,

01:28:09.150 --> 01:28:11.280
interoperable systems,

01:28:11.280 --> 01:28:13.410
a single data architecture,

01:28:13.410 --> 01:28:15.870
an IT infrastructure that actually works

01:28:15.870 --> 01:28:17.400
at the point of care.

