01:45:09.090 --> 01:45:11.100
So let's go on to the last item.

01:45:11.100 --> 01:45:14.430
Actually I'm delighted
actually we've got Adrian James

01:45:14.430 --> 01:45:15.480
and Mark Ewins.

01:45:15.480 --> 01:45:17.760
So Adrian, National Medical Director

01:45:17.760 --> 01:45:20.040
for Mental Health and Neurodiversity

01:45:20.040 --> 01:45:22.230
and Mark, our Head of Mental Health.

01:45:22.230 --> 01:45:25.590
So welcome and over to
you really, on the topic.

01:45:25.590 --> 01:45:28.980
It's, "Supporting
Strategy and Actions Taken

01:45:28.980 --> 01:45:30.810
to Improve Quality and Community Services

01:45:30.810 --> 01:45:32.610
for Mental Health" is the title.

01:45:32.610 --> 01:45:33.900
And you provided us
with a very good paper,

01:45:33.900 --> 01:45:35.100
but you want to draw out the main points

01:45:35.100 --> 01:45:37.200
and then you've seen the way it works

01:45:37.200 --> 01:45:39.950
and I'll attempt to keep it
under some form of control.

01:45:41.040 --> 01:45:42.600
- Well thank you, Chair.

01:45:42.600 --> 01:45:45.030
I'm very pleased to be
here addressing the board

01:45:45.030 --> 01:45:47.370
about this important issue.

01:45:47.370 --> 01:45:49.620
I will assume that the
paper has been read,

01:45:49.620 --> 01:45:51.720
but I'll summarise the main points

01:45:51.720 --> 01:45:53.670
and the actions that we are taking.

01:45:53.670 --> 01:45:56.850
So what's the patient group
that we are looking at here?

01:45:56.850 --> 01:45:59.700
They are those with
serious mental illness.

01:45:59.700 --> 01:46:02.730
So that's mostly those who
are experiencing psychosis.

01:46:02.730 --> 01:46:05.490
Mostly of those would be schizophrenia,

01:46:05.490 --> 01:46:06.960
bipolar disorder,

01:46:06.960 --> 01:46:10.800
or some with emotionally
unstable personality disorder.

01:46:10.800 --> 01:46:14.910
There are approximately a
million people who experience

01:46:14.910 --> 01:46:16.320
those conditions

01:46:16.320 --> 01:46:18.900
and a lot of them would
experience hallucinations,

01:46:18.900 --> 01:46:23.370
delusions and paranoia
and be really very unwell

01:46:23.370 --> 01:46:24.930
with their condition and it will affect

01:46:24.930 --> 01:46:26.370
all aspects of their life,

01:46:26.370 --> 01:46:29.610
their education, work and family life.

01:46:29.610 --> 01:46:31.560
So what do they need from the NHS?

01:46:31.560 --> 01:46:33.570
They need an early diagnosis.

01:46:33.570 --> 01:46:35.100
They need early intervention.

01:46:35.100 --> 01:46:36.990
The earlier we intervene,

01:46:36.990 --> 01:46:39.630
the better the outcomes that they have.

01:46:39.630 --> 01:46:42.510
They need conventional community treatment

01:46:42.510 --> 01:46:45.300
in partnership with
primary care colleagues.

01:46:45.300 --> 01:46:48.780
And most will be managed
in those community teams

01:46:48.780 --> 01:46:50.970
without further specialist input.

01:46:50.970 --> 01:46:53.190
But some will get into crisis.

01:46:53.190 --> 01:46:55.620
They will need alternative to admissions

01:46:55.620 --> 01:46:58.530
so that they can get support at home.

01:46:58.530 --> 01:47:01.050
Mental health services
has been running wards

01:47:01.050 --> 01:47:03.300
in the community for decades

01:47:03.300 --> 01:47:07.380
in terms of the intensive
support we give people at home.

01:47:07.380 --> 01:47:11.100
But also we need an inpatient care as well

01:47:11.100 --> 01:47:13.620
for those who find it
difficult to recover.

01:47:13.620 --> 01:47:15.990
We have rehabilitation services

01:47:15.990 --> 01:47:17.310
for those who've been in contact

01:47:17.310 --> 01:47:18.960
with the criminal justice system,

01:47:18.960 --> 01:47:22.680
forensic services who also
operate in the community.

01:47:22.680 --> 01:47:25.140
And we also have assertive
outreach services.

01:47:25.140 --> 01:47:27.180
And I'll come back to those

01:47:27.180 --> 01:47:30.150
because there has been a
particular focus on those

01:47:30.150 --> 01:47:32.070
in the last few months.

01:47:32.070 --> 01:47:34.830
So what are we doing to improve quality?

01:47:34.830 --> 01:47:36.360
There were a number of plans

01:47:36.360 --> 01:47:37.770
with the long-term plan,

01:47:37.770 --> 01:47:42.030
a billion extra invested into
community mental health care.

01:47:42.030 --> 01:47:46.110
And as a result of that, we
have 71% of those experiencing

01:47:46.110 --> 01:47:48.120
a first episode of psychosis

01:47:48.120 --> 01:47:49.740
being seen within two weeks.

01:47:49.740 --> 01:47:53.160
That's really a pretty stunning statistic.

01:47:53.160 --> 01:47:56.070
We have a programme at the
moment around the culture of care

01:47:56.070 --> 01:47:57.780
within mental health services.

01:47:57.780 --> 01:47:59.940
Every single trust in the country

01:47:59.940 --> 01:48:02.490
is signed up to this and are engaged.

01:48:02.490 --> 01:48:04.920
We have a number of quality
improvement programmes

01:48:04.920 --> 01:48:07.170
that have been very
successful, for example,

01:48:07.170 --> 01:48:09.450
in reducing restrictive practises

01:48:09.450 --> 01:48:11.700
and improving sexual safety.

01:48:11.700 --> 01:48:13.140
And we're currently piloting,

01:48:13.140 --> 01:48:14.640
just launched this,

01:48:14.640 --> 01:48:18.330
six new pilots looking at models of care

01:48:18.330 --> 01:48:20.130
that are different to
the ones that we provide

01:48:20.130 --> 01:48:21.180
at the moment.

01:48:21.180 --> 01:48:23.100
On a neighbourhood basis,

01:48:23.100 --> 01:48:26.370
population around 50,000 individuals

01:48:26.370 --> 01:48:29.730
taking the best learning
from all over the world

01:48:29.730 --> 01:48:34.530
with open access partnership
with local community groups

01:48:34.530 --> 01:48:36.123
and the voluntary sector,

01:48:37.050 --> 01:48:40.590
co-production being central
to everything that's done

01:48:40.590 --> 01:48:44.400
and building up trusting
relationships that are lasting

01:48:44.400 --> 01:48:46.770
so there's continuity of care.

01:48:46.770 --> 01:48:48.690
But we, and we also recently,

01:48:48.690 --> 01:48:50.850
we've got the review of
the Mental Health Act

01:48:50.850 --> 01:48:54.090
and legislation that will be
introduced into parliament.

01:48:54.090 --> 01:48:57.120
And again, a lot of
that is about improving

01:48:57.120 --> 01:48:58.320
mental health care,

01:48:58.320 --> 01:49:02.070
improving the experience
that our service users have,

01:49:02.070 --> 01:49:04.290
and particularly addressing inequalities

01:49:04.290 --> 01:49:07.110
that have already been
mentioned in this meeting.

01:49:07.110 --> 01:49:08.730
So we have a number of challenges,

01:49:08.730 --> 01:49:11.820
increased demand, increased referrals,

01:49:11.820 --> 01:49:13.590
long waits in emergency departments

01:49:13.590 --> 01:49:15.300
have already been mentioned.

01:49:15.300 --> 01:49:17.790
We have high numbers of
out of area placements

01:49:17.790 --> 01:49:19.500
for those who just require

01:49:19.500 --> 01:49:22.770
conventional inpatient psychiatric care.

01:49:22.770 --> 01:49:27.060
We have high occupancy rates of above 95%

01:49:27.060 --> 01:49:28.920
and staffing challenges.

01:49:28.920 --> 01:49:30.240
But with all those challenges,

01:49:30.240 --> 01:49:33.090
we now have a million more people

01:49:33.090 --> 01:49:36.150
who are being seen by specialist
mental health services

01:49:36.150 --> 01:49:39.060
year-on-year than were
seen before the pandemic.

01:49:39.060 --> 01:49:42.630
And again, that's a
tribute really to our staff

01:49:42.630 --> 01:49:45.090
who have redoubled their efforts really

01:49:45.090 --> 01:49:47.070
to provide that care.

01:49:47.070 --> 01:49:50.280
We have had a number of
serious, untoward incidents

01:49:50.280 --> 01:49:52.500
that have occurred that have caused

01:49:52.500 --> 01:49:56.490
understandable extreme
concern from the public

01:49:56.490 --> 01:49:58.290
and those working in services

01:49:58.290 --> 01:50:00.360
and those who use services.

01:50:00.360 --> 01:50:03.870
And one of the ones that
was particularly shocking

01:50:03.870 --> 01:50:07.140
and tragic were the death
of three individuals

01:50:07.140 --> 01:50:08.220
in Nottingham.

01:50:08.220 --> 01:50:10.320
And of course our thoughts must be

01:50:10.320 --> 01:50:14.670
with friends and family of
those who tragically died.

01:50:14.670 --> 01:50:16.170
One can't imagine the impact

01:50:16.170 --> 01:50:19.890
that it's had on their lives
and everything that they do.

01:50:19.890 --> 01:50:22.230
And we owe it to them to do all we can

01:50:22.230 --> 01:50:25.920
to minimise the chance
of these tragic incidents

01:50:25.920 --> 01:50:27.303
happening again.

01:50:28.140 --> 01:50:31.380
So that particular group
that we are looking at,

01:50:31.380 --> 01:50:32.940
we, they are those that require

01:50:32.940 --> 01:50:35.310
intensive community treatment.

01:50:35.310 --> 01:50:36.960
So the profile of those individuals,

01:50:36.960 --> 01:50:40.080
they would normally have
a psychotic mental illness

01:50:40.080 --> 01:50:41.100
as part of that.

01:50:41.100 --> 01:50:42.669
I've already mentioned delusions,

01:50:42.669 --> 01:50:44.730
hallucinations,

01:50:44.730 --> 01:50:48.270
and these appear as reality.

01:50:48.270 --> 01:50:50.730
So I'm talking to you, you
are hearing what I'm saying.

01:50:50.730 --> 01:50:52.050
You see me in the room

01:50:52.050 --> 01:50:55.230
and if somebody said to
you later on I wasn't here,

01:50:55.230 --> 01:50:56.550
I wasn't saying those things,

01:50:56.550 --> 01:50:58.500
you'd find that hard to believe.

01:50:58.500 --> 01:51:01.140
And if somebody was saying
that was what was happening,

01:51:01.140 --> 01:51:03.330
then you would probably question them

01:51:03.330 --> 01:51:06.240
and you might not be
inclined to engage with them.

01:51:06.240 --> 01:51:09.570
So this is a very challenging
situation for patients

01:51:09.570 --> 01:51:10.710
and for staff.

01:51:10.710 --> 01:51:13.410
And again, I want to pay
tribute to patients and staff

01:51:13.410 --> 01:51:15.810
who work very hard to
build those relationships

01:51:15.810 --> 01:51:17.610
and build up those trust,

01:51:17.610 --> 01:51:19.650
trust the trusting relationship.

01:51:19.650 --> 01:51:22.530
But unfortunately this
can lead to some patients

01:51:22.530 --> 01:51:25.050
disengaging from services,

01:51:25.050 --> 01:51:27.450
not seeing, not coming to appointments,

01:51:27.450 --> 01:51:29.640
not taking their medication.

01:51:29.640 --> 01:51:32.850
And we know in those patients
who experience psychosis,

01:51:32.850 --> 01:51:34.860
this can be highly risky for them

01:51:34.860 --> 01:51:38.280
and sometimes risky for
other people as well.

01:51:38.280 --> 01:51:42.420
So what's been happening as
a result of these concerns?

01:51:42.420 --> 01:51:46.290
There are very specific actions
in relation to Nottingham.

01:51:46.290 --> 01:51:49.350
The Nottingham trust are in
the highest level of scrutiny.

01:51:49.350 --> 01:51:51.720
There's been a local investigation,

01:51:51.720 --> 01:51:53.490
the CQC reported,

01:51:53.490 --> 01:51:55.800
those actions have all been looked at

01:51:55.800 --> 01:51:58.470
both locally and nationally.

01:51:58.470 --> 01:52:02.580
And we're expecting the
Independent Homicide Inquiry Report

01:52:02.580 --> 01:52:05.910
to report in the next few months.

01:52:05.910 --> 01:52:08.070
But we didn't want to
just look at one area

01:52:08.070 --> 01:52:09.930
because we wanted to make sure

01:52:09.930 --> 01:52:12.855
that we looked at all parts of the country

01:52:12.855 --> 01:52:14.910
to see what the challenges were.

01:52:14.910 --> 01:52:17.400
So on the 26th of July,

01:52:17.400 --> 01:52:21.420
we asked all ICBs, all 42 ICBs

01:52:21.420 --> 01:52:25.410
to look at their approach to
assertive community treatment

01:52:25.410 --> 01:52:27.150
for this patient group.

01:52:27.150 --> 01:52:28.410
What were their policies?

01:52:28.410 --> 01:52:30.780
Could they identify these individuals?

01:52:30.780 --> 01:52:33.480
Could they actually
monitor those individuals?

01:52:33.480 --> 01:52:35.040
What are the gaps in services?

01:52:35.040 --> 01:52:36.570
What are the challenges?

01:52:36.570 --> 01:52:39.690
And what next steps are they taking

01:52:39.690 --> 01:52:42.210
and would they like to take?

01:52:42.210 --> 01:52:47.100
And we wanted them to report
by the 30th of September.

01:52:47.100 --> 01:52:49.500
We also issued a renewed guidance

01:52:49.500 --> 01:52:51.360
around assertive outreach treatment.

01:52:51.360 --> 01:52:53.340
So this is about policies.

01:52:53.340 --> 01:52:56.130
One of the particular
concerns we discovered was

01:52:56.130 --> 01:52:58.140
that there was a policy in Nottingham

01:52:58.140 --> 01:53:00.960
where if somebody didn't
appear to an appointment

01:53:00.960 --> 01:53:02.220
on a couple of occasions,

01:53:02.220 --> 01:53:04.410
they were discharged from the service.

01:53:04.410 --> 01:53:07.230
That may be appropriate
for some patient groups,

01:53:07.230 --> 01:53:08.940
but for this patient group,

01:53:08.940 --> 01:53:11.730
it causes a huge amount of risk

01:53:11.730 --> 01:53:14.160
and is clearly not right,

01:53:14.160 --> 01:53:15.930
but also issued new guidance

01:53:15.930 --> 01:53:17.310
around use of medication,

01:53:17.310 --> 01:53:21.090
particularly Depo medication
and use of legal frameworks.

01:53:21.090 --> 01:53:24.000
Obviously, particularly
the Mental Health Act.

01:53:24.000 --> 01:53:28.260
The initial feedback that we
had was about a third of trusts

01:53:28.260 --> 01:53:32.700
had assertive outreach teams
dedicated to this approach.

01:53:32.700 --> 01:53:35.070
A third had a particular approach,

01:53:35.070 --> 01:53:37.350
maybe dealt with by a different team,

01:53:37.350 --> 01:53:40.200
and a third didn't have
a very specific approach

01:53:40.200 --> 01:53:42.390
for those individuals.

01:53:42.390 --> 01:53:45.030
We've now had the responses from all ICBs,

01:53:45.030 --> 01:53:48.870
I'm delighted to say, 42 out of 42.

01:53:48.870 --> 01:53:52.800
We need to analyse the
feedback that we've had

01:53:52.800 --> 01:53:55.020
and they, we have seen
some very good practise

01:53:55.020 --> 01:53:55.980
that's going on.

01:53:55.980 --> 01:53:58.440
Highly effective assertive outreach teams

01:53:58.440 --> 01:54:00.390
providing excellent care,

01:54:00.390 --> 01:54:04.260
reducing risk for patients
and for the general public.

01:54:04.260 --> 01:54:07.830
But we've also had highlighted
significant challenges,

01:54:07.830 --> 01:54:11.640
a lack of a specific team,
workforce challenges,

01:54:11.640 --> 01:54:13.560
challenges with data.

01:54:13.560 --> 01:54:14.790
And they've also requested

01:54:14.790 --> 01:54:17.880
as part of that more central guidance,

01:54:17.880 --> 01:54:20.370
more central training,

01:54:20.370 --> 01:54:24.030
sharing good practise that is out there

01:54:24.030 --> 01:54:26.070
and a real look

01:54:26.070 --> 01:54:27.540
at all parts of the system.

01:54:27.540 --> 01:54:30.240
Those, all the requirements
that people with

01:54:30.240 --> 01:54:31.920
severe mental illness have

01:54:31.920 --> 01:54:33.540
in order to provide the best care

01:54:33.540 --> 01:54:36.360
so we don't just look at
one part of the system

01:54:36.360 --> 01:54:39.600
and shift the risk from
one area to another.

01:54:39.600 --> 01:54:41.580
So the ICBs have been asked to report

01:54:41.580 --> 01:54:43.740
through their public boards.

01:54:43.740 --> 01:54:46.380
That will be being done
within the next few months

01:54:46.380 --> 01:54:49.050
about what the situation is locally

01:54:49.050 --> 01:54:51.450
and what their plans are.

01:54:51.450 --> 01:54:54.210
Regional teams are also reviewing

01:54:54.210 --> 01:54:56.190
the ICB returns

01:54:56.190 --> 01:54:57.330
and they'll be reviewing those

01:54:57.330 --> 01:54:59.610
by the 14th of October.

01:54:59.610 --> 01:55:02.310
Centrally, we'll be
following up the concerns,

01:55:02.310 --> 01:55:05.820
actions and what we are
doing in six and 12 months

01:55:05.820 --> 01:55:10.820
and obviously briefing the
executive team about that.

01:55:11.040 --> 01:55:13.860
We are also reviewing the learning

01:55:13.860 --> 01:55:17.730
that comes from Serious Untoward
Incident Investigations,

01:55:17.730 --> 01:55:19.800
particularly homicide reports.

01:55:19.800 --> 01:55:23.190
And Aidan Fowler is leading
a piece of work on that.

01:55:23.190 --> 01:55:25.250
I'll be sitting on the group,

01:55:25.250 --> 01:55:28.440
so to make sure that
we both learn lessons,

01:55:28.440 --> 01:55:31.650
but actually that we respond to them

01:55:31.650 --> 01:55:33.750
for the clinical care of patients.

01:55:33.750 --> 01:55:35.940
And finally we're going to be undertaking

01:55:35.940 --> 01:55:37.530
a longer term review

01:55:37.530 --> 01:55:39.120
of all parts of the system

01:55:39.120 --> 01:55:42.150
so that we make sure we
have a joined up approach

01:55:42.150 --> 01:55:44.340
to everything that's happening.

01:55:44.340 --> 01:55:48.510
And we heard earlier on about
the emergency departments.

01:55:48.510 --> 01:55:51.210
We have more longer waits for those

01:55:51.210 --> 01:55:53.820
with severe mental illness
than any other group.

01:55:53.820 --> 01:55:56.940
That has a knock-on effect
to the rest of the system

01:55:56.940 --> 01:55:59.760
as well as not providing optimal care.

01:55:59.760 --> 01:56:01.620
And I think, Mark, you
were just going to say

01:56:01.620 --> 01:56:04.560
something about the urgent
and emergency response,

01:56:04.560 --> 01:56:06.540
but it's very important that we get

01:56:06.540 --> 01:56:10.050
all parts of the system
working in harmony.

01:56:10.050 --> 01:56:12.270
There's really great
practise that's going on.

01:56:12.270 --> 01:56:14.970
I visited the South West Ambulance Service

01:56:14.970 --> 01:56:16.650
just a couple of months ago

01:56:16.650 --> 01:56:18.810
and they've got fabulous systems

01:56:18.810 --> 01:56:20.850
working very closely with the police,

01:56:20.850 --> 01:56:24.000
with local mental health
services across primary care.

01:56:24.000 --> 01:56:26.190
And I think there's a
real opportunity here.

01:56:26.190 --> 01:56:27.330
There is good practise,

01:56:27.330 --> 01:56:28.710
there's real willingness

01:56:28.710 --> 01:56:30.360
across the whole of medicine

01:56:30.360 --> 01:56:33.240
to address this and make a real difference

01:56:33.240 --> 01:56:35.790
and reduce significantly

01:56:35.790 --> 01:56:39.300
the chance of these tragic
events happening again.

01:56:39.300 --> 01:56:40.317
- So Adrian, thank you very much.

01:56:40.317 --> 01:56:42.360
I'm going to go around, you've seen that,

01:56:42.360 --> 01:56:43.653
you've seen how it works.

01:56:44.640 --> 01:56:46.249
So we're going to go
this way round this time.

01:56:46.249 --> 01:56:47.250
- [Adrian] Mark, do you want to just say?

01:56:47.250 --> 01:56:48.374
- Mark, sorry, do you want to?

01:56:48.374 --> 01:56:49.649
- Well, I'm happy just to answer.

01:56:49.649 --> 01:56:51.600
I know some of the early
questions about the,

01:56:51.600 --> 01:56:53.640
what we're doing in mental
health to support resilience

01:56:53.640 --> 01:56:54.473
this winter.

01:56:54.473 --> 01:56:56.100
And I just wanted to
say there's three things

01:56:56.100 --> 01:56:58.350
that we're putting in
place ahead of this winter

01:56:58.350 --> 01:57:00.180
that were not in place last year.

01:57:00.180 --> 01:57:02.970
One of them is the
establishment of 111 option,

01:57:02.970 --> 01:57:06.120
mental health option that
was not in place last year.

01:57:06.120 --> 01:57:07.740
That is going to be a game-changer

01:57:07.740 --> 01:57:09.660
in terms of enabling patients

01:57:09.660 --> 01:57:12.810
to access the alternative
crisis infrastructure

01:57:12.810 --> 01:57:15.000
that we've developed for mental health

01:57:15.000 --> 01:57:16.530
through the Longterm Plan.

01:57:16.530 --> 01:57:17.580
The second is the rollout

01:57:17.580 --> 01:57:19.560
of mental health response vehicles.

01:57:19.560 --> 01:57:22.080
They were not being rolled
out until February this year.

01:57:22.080 --> 01:57:25.590
We've now have about 58 being
made available to systems.

01:57:25.590 --> 01:57:29.040
Some of the evaluations that
we've seen on similar vehicles

01:57:29.040 --> 01:57:30.410
have had conveyance rates to ED

01:57:30.410 --> 01:57:33.780
at 19% compared to their comparisons

01:57:33.780 --> 01:57:37.980
of 54% for those that didn't
have mental health available

01:57:37.980 --> 01:57:39.390
at the scene.

01:57:39.390 --> 01:57:42.720
And thirdly is establishing
OPAL for mental health.

01:57:42.720 --> 01:57:45.510
That's going to bring daily
and weekly information

01:57:45.510 --> 01:57:47.370
on the pressures that are facing,

01:57:47.370 --> 01:57:50.580
not just in ED but also the
waits that we're seeing for beds

01:57:50.580 --> 01:57:53.070
in the community and in things like homes.

01:57:53.070 --> 01:57:54.600
That will make it much easier

01:57:54.600 --> 01:57:57.480
to actually manage the
pressures we're seeing.

01:57:57.480 --> 01:57:58.860
- Thank you.

01:57:58.860 --> 01:58:01.890
Very good, Mark, very good,
very good additions, I think.

01:58:01.890 --> 01:58:05.760
- Can I just make a point
around comms on the 111 service?

01:58:05.760 --> 01:58:09.660
So how are we going to get that
message over to the public?

01:58:09.660 --> 01:58:11.137
Is that part of the winter plan?

01:58:11.137 --> 01:58:11.970
- [Person Off-Camera] It's out, isn't it?

01:58:11.970 --> 01:58:15.420
- It is out, yes, and I know
it's now trying to be included

01:58:15.420 --> 01:58:17.250
within the broader NHS winter plan.

01:58:17.250 --> 01:58:20.068
- It's on the news it's, but
yes, on social media news,

01:58:20.068 --> 01:58:20.901
it's out.

01:58:20.901 --> 01:58:22.440
It's pretty strong brand, I think.

01:58:22.440 --> 01:58:24.570
- Okay, I'm going to quickly, so Robert,

01:58:24.570 --> 01:58:26.823
and then I'll just go round the, Robert.

01:58:27.720 --> 01:58:29.400
- Fantastic work.

01:58:29.400 --> 01:58:30.630
Two quick questions.

01:58:30.630 --> 01:58:33.540
One is, as I'm sure you well know,

01:58:33.540 --> 01:58:35.280
if you have the misfortune to suffer

01:58:35.280 --> 01:58:36.660
from severe long-term mental illness,

01:58:36.660 --> 01:58:39.750
your lifespan is shortened
by about 17 years.

01:58:39.750 --> 01:58:41.760
That's physical comorbidities mostly.

01:58:41.760 --> 01:58:46.110
So how well in place is the management

01:58:46.110 --> 01:58:48.060
of the physical comorbidities
that these patients

01:58:48.060 --> 01:58:49.530
also suffer as question one.

01:58:49.530 --> 01:58:52.470
Question two, in terms of
detecting early relapse,

01:58:52.470 --> 01:58:54.840
wearables seem to be
a promising technology

01:58:54.840 --> 01:58:57.000
to detect disturbed sleep patterns

01:58:57.000 --> 01:58:58.950
or movement disorders and so on.

01:58:58.950 --> 01:59:01.110
How widely are wearables being used

01:59:01.110 --> 01:59:03.297
to monitor these patients
in the community?

01:59:04.203 --> 01:59:05.453
- Going around,

01:59:06.810 --> 01:59:08.013
any other comments?

01:59:09.300 --> 01:59:11.520
- Mark?
- Just a general,

01:59:11.520 --> 01:59:12.810
to give us context,

01:59:12.810 --> 01:59:15.110
there's clearly a massive
explosion in demand.

01:59:16.170 --> 01:59:19.653
At current rates, how
does this roll forward?

01:59:20.730 --> 01:59:23.880
i.e. there's clearly
capacity shortfall today.

01:59:23.880 --> 01:59:25.050
If it carries on,

01:59:25.050 --> 01:59:28.170
the demand grows at the
rate it's growing today,

01:59:28.170 --> 01:59:30.003
where do we end up long term?

01:59:31.410 --> 01:59:32.670
- And if I had a,

01:59:32.670 --> 01:59:33.503
so that's that question.

01:59:33.503 --> 01:59:35.670
If I had a, it's more of a comment

01:59:35.670 --> 01:59:37.890
and it's going to be a
rather silly comment I think,

01:59:37.890 --> 01:59:40.260
but there are a couple of areas in here

01:59:40.260 --> 01:59:43.380
where you are going to
do effectively a study

01:59:43.380 --> 01:59:45.180
or we're going to roll out a pilot.

01:59:45.180 --> 01:59:46.320
It just struck me reading

01:59:46.320 --> 01:59:50.940
as a completely uneducated
layman in this field,

01:59:50.940 --> 01:59:55.440
that the evaluation periods
look quite a long way away

01:59:55.440 --> 01:59:57.330
before we, so the question is,

01:59:57.330 --> 01:59:59.190
before we get to the end date,

01:59:59.190 --> 02:00:01.590
are we going to be taking
any early learnings

02:00:01.590 --> 02:00:02.423
and trying to get,

02:00:02.423 --> 02:00:05.370
I'll call it quick wins
or quicker interventions?

02:00:05.370 --> 02:00:08.070
Or do we wait for the end
of a two-year evaluation

02:00:08.070 --> 02:00:10.020
before we then remeet and then,

02:00:10.020 --> 02:00:12.000
or you then remeet and then decide

02:00:12.000 --> 02:00:13.230
how you roll that out?

02:00:13.230 --> 02:00:16.590
So can we be faster than
the paper's suggestion?

02:00:16.590 --> 02:00:19.770
I think there are two or
three examples in here.

02:00:19.770 --> 02:00:21.480
All very, they all seem very sensible.

02:00:21.480 --> 02:00:23.850
It's just the pace at
which we would deliver

02:00:23.850 --> 02:00:25.080
an intervention.

02:00:25.080 --> 02:00:26.100
So do you want to take a,

02:00:26.100 --> 02:00:27.360
have a go at those questions?

02:00:27.360 --> 02:00:28.513
That'd be great.

02:00:28.513 --> 02:00:30.420
- Okay, so in relation to physical health

02:00:30.420 --> 02:00:31.380
and mental disorders,

02:00:31.380 --> 02:00:34.620
you rightly say this is a real challenge.

02:00:34.620 --> 02:00:36.030
I'm in the very lucky position

02:00:36.030 --> 02:00:39.030
that I sit within the medical directorate

02:00:39.030 --> 02:00:40.830
rather than the mental health directorate.

02:00:40.830 --> 02:00:43.380
And I think it's been
recognised by NHS England

02:00:43.380 --> 02:00:45.060
there's been a bit of a gap.

02:00:45.060 --> 02:00:48.090
Steve will know that
when I go to any meeting

02:00:48.090 --> 02:00:49.800
that when we talk about cancer,

02:00:49.800 --> 02:00:52.020
when we talk about respiratory,

02:00:52.020 --> 02:00:54.420
when we talk about cardiovascular,

02:00:54.420 --> 02:00:56.550
I always ask what are the programmes

02:00:56.550 --> 02:00:58.800
for those who have the greatest need

02:00:58.800 --> 02:01:00.300
and the poorest outcome?

02:01:00.300 --> 02:01:02.790
And often those patients,
they're people with

02:01:02.790 --> 02:01:04.320
severe mental illness

02:01:04.320 --> 02:01:07.890
and let's not forget learning
disability and autism.

02:01:07.890 --> 02:01:09.930
There are health checks,

02:01:09.930 --> 02:01:12.900
the numbers have improved recently,

02:01:12.900 --> 02:01:14.700
but we are not at the
level of health checks

02:01:14.700 --> 02:01:16.260
that we would like to have.

02:01:16.260 --> 02:01:17.880
And there's an additional push

02:01:17.880 --> 02:01:19.860
in order to move that forward.

02:01:19.860 --> 02:01:22.080
In terms of wearables,

02:01:22.080 --> 02:01:24.780
this is something of great
interest to all of us.

02:01:24.780 --> 02:01:26.220
There are a huge number of people

02:01:26.220 --> 02:01:28.680
who are monitoring their own mental health

02:01:28.680 --> 02:01:33.210
using apps, looking at
their own risk factors,

02:01:33.210 --> 02:01:35.100
accumulation of risk factors

02:01:35.100 --> 02:01:37.410
and linking those to friends and family

02:01:37.410 --> 02:01:39.660
or even to care workers.

02:01:39.660 --> 02:01:42.780
There's a huge amount
of work that we can do

02:01:42.780 --> 02:01:45.240
to further those sorts of approaches

02:01:45.240 --> 02:01:46.920
and we do need to make sure

02:01:46.920 --> 02:01:49.620
that they are evidence-based.

02:01:49.620 --> 02:01:53.100
Now the question about
what will happen to demand,

02:01:53.100 --> 02:01:56.790
I think COVID did do
something quite extraordinary

02:01:56.790 --> 02:01:58.530
in terms of demand,

02:01:58.530 --> 02:02:00.840
but we have also other pressures concerned

02:02:00.840 --> 02:02:03.510
about social media

02:02:03.510 --> 02:02:05.550
and particularly young people

02:02:05.550 --> 02:02:08.550
and there are programmes
looking to address that.

02:02:08.550 --> 02:02:10.830
So I think we have to hope

02:02:10.830 --> 02:02:12.930
that in terms of some of the prevention

02:02:12.930 --> 02:02:14.250
that we can actually do something

02:02:14.250 --> 02:02:17.490
about the ever-escalating levels.

02:02:17.490 --> 02:02:20.460
I don't think that every,
anybody is suggesting

02:02:20.460 --> 02:02:25.460
that the demand will increase
in the way that it has done.

02:02:25.800 --> 02:02:28.170
I couldn't say that won't happen,

02:02:28.170 --> 02:02:30.790
but I don't suspect that it will carry on

02:02:31.830 --> 02:02:33.600
in the way that it has.

02:02:33.600 --> 02:02:37.740
But if you look at global
disease burden at the moment,

02:02:37.740 --> 02:02:42.360
the global disease burden
of mental illness is 23%.

02:02:42.360 --> 02:02:46.740
So of all, if you look at the
overall impact of illnesses,

02:02:46.740 --> 02:02:48.600
so that's more than in cancer,

02:02:48.600 --> 02:02:51.390
it's more than cardiovascular.

02:02:51.390 --> 02:02:54.090
If we carry on in the same way globally,

02:02:54.090 --> 02:02:55.950
and in fact we are pretty
well in the same position

02:02:55.950 --> 02:02:57.240
in the UK,

02:02:57.240 --> 02:02:58.570
the global

02:03:00.660 --> 02:03:02.790
disease burden will be 43%

02:03:02.790 --> 02:03:07.790
unless we can actually do
something to reduce the demand.

02:03:09.450 --> 02:03:13.260
It partly goes up because of
the success in other areas.

02:03:13.260 --> 02:03:17.880
And so we need to have the same
sort of focus on prevention

02:03:17.880 --> 02:03:20.520
and early intervention in
the mental health sector

02:03:20.520 --> 02:03:23.820
that perhaps we've had in other areas.

02:03:23.820 --> 02:03:27.938
And in relation to Chair's
point about learning,

02:03:29.005 --> 02:03:31.200
I very much want to,

02:03:31.200 --> 02:03:34.290
we've set a two-year timescale.

02:03:34.290 --> 02:03:36.613
I want to underpromise and overdeliver,

02:03:37.680 --> 02:03:40.260
but I'm aware that there
are some real pinch points

02:03:40.260 --> 02:03:43.230
that we need to have
something of value back.

02:03:43.230 --> 02:03:46.680
Key to this is the 10-year
plan and I think we,

02:03:46.680 --> 02:03:50.070
looking at timescales a week or so ago,

02:03:50.070 --> 02:03:52.500
there's probably a time in around March

02:03:52.500 --> 02:03:55.230
that we need to have
something to come back

02:03:55.230 --> 02:03:57.390
and might be optimistic
that we will have something

02:03:57.390 --> 02:03:58.620
to say then.

02:03:58.620 --> 02:04:02.190
And then I think around
early autumn next year

02:04:02.190 --> 02:04:03.720
in terms of all this,

02:04:03.720 --> 02:04:05.190
the spending cycles,

02:04:05.190 --> 02:04:08.310
we will also need to have
something back then as well.

02:04:08.310 --> 02:04:10.170
So I'm very much aware of that.

02:04:10.170 --> 02:04:13.380
I don't want to wait
right to the very end,

02:04:13.380 --> 02:04:15.030
but I do want to make sure

02:04:15.030 --> 02:04:19.590
that we have a really proper,
evidence-based look at this,

02:04:19.590 --> 02:04:21.060
that we engage services,

02:04:21.060 --> 02:04:23.250
we engage service users

02:04:23.250 --> 02:04:24.900
so we make sure that we get it right.

02:04:24.900 --> 02:04:28.110
- So can I just, I'm going
to ask Duncan and Steve

02:04:28.110 --> 02:04:30.930
if they have any questions
in a moment just for,

02:04:30.930 --> 02:04:31.763
before I do that,

02:04:31.763 --> 02:04:33.690
the only other aspect

02:04:33.690 --> 02:04:37.870
which I just felt slightly
unnerved about at the end

02:04:39.060 --> 02:04:39.893
reading the paper,

02:04:39.893 --> 02:04:42.060
it's where you talk
about inpatient services

02:04:42.060 --> 02:04:43.800
and then wider service dependencies.

02:04:43.800 --> 02:04:46.080
And you're very clear about the challenge

02:04:46.080 --> 02:04:48.420
and I prompted it because
I know Mary Watkins,

02:04:48.420 --> 02:04:51.210
Baroness Watkins has been
asking very much on this point

02:04:51.210 --> 02:04:53.340
about available beds.

02:04:53.340 --> 02:04:55.710
And so what we see is we have 24,000 beds.

02:04:55.710 --> 02:05:00.210
I think it costs about 520
units of provision, I think,

02:05:00.210 --> 02:05:02.850
many of which I think are
private sector provision.

02:05:02.850 --> 02:05:04.830
That's absolutely fine.

02:05:04.830 --> 02:05:06.495
But the issue is, and you alluded to it

02:05:06.495 --> 02:05:07.440
in your opening remarks,

02:05:07.440 --> 02:05:11.190
it's running at a 95% capacity.

02:05:11.190 --> 02:05:14.010
And the question I think
is how do we think about

02:05:14.010 --> 02:05:16.560
actually because that doesn't seem at all

02:05:16.560 --> 02:05:19.710
a sort of a sustainable
level at which to run.

02:05:19.710 --> 02:05:21.360
You've also got the staff shortages point

02:05:21.360 --> 02:05:22.230
which are running at double,

02:05:22.230 --> 02:05:24.960
I call it double the average
in terms of vacancies.

02:05:24.960 --> 02:05:27.030
So pressure on mental
health nurses and medics.

02:05:27.030 --> 02:05:31.200
So getting enough people trained
and working in the system

02:05:31.200 --> 02:05:33.750
and then just the paragraph
on wider service dependencies.

02:05:33.750 --> 02:05:34.583
I mean I don't,

02:05:34.583 --> 02:05:36.180
so we don't disagree

02:05:36.180 --> 02:05:38.280
but actually sort of hangs out there

02:05:38.280 --> 02:05:40.560
in terms of many of
these services themselves

02:05:40.560 --> 02:05:42.837
are struggling with funding and support.

02:05:42.837 --> 02:05:46.201
And so the question I think
is how does one coordinate

02:05:46.201 --> 02:05:51.201
in this necessary
multiplicity of, you know,

02:05:51.330 --> 02:05:53.340
agents working together in this space

02:05:53.340 --> 02:05:54.510
or agencies,

02:05:54.510 --> 02:05:57.060
but actually many of
whom are significantly

02:05:57.060 --> 02:05:59.370
I think under pressure
and financial pressure.

02:05:59.370 --> 02:06:01.020
So when I read those I was, you know,

02:06:01.020 --> 02:06:03.003
I think there's a, you
work in an area of real,

02:06:03.003 --> 02:06:05.190
it's not just that the mental
health is a real challenge,

02:06:05.190 --> 02:06:08.610
but actually in terms of the
capacity to deliver service,

02:06:08.610 --> 02:06:11.250
it does remain of very
challenging picture you give us.

02:06:11.250 --> 02:06:15.270
But that's, but you can either
comment on that or ignore it.

02:06:15.270 --> 02:06:17.790
But I'm going to ask
Duncan first and then Steve

02:06:17.790 --> 02:06:19.470
and then Vin very quickly to come through.

02:06:19.470 --> 02:06:21.540
So Duncan, do you have any
reflections or questions?

02:06:21.540 --> 02:06:24.060
- Yeah, I mean we've
spoken about this before,

02:06:24.060 --> 02:06:25.620
but particularly the
importance of learning

02:06:25.620 --> 02:06:27.990
and learning from the homicide reviews

02:06:27.990 --> 02:06:31.320
that happen and tragic cases when they,

02:06:31.320 --> 02:06:33.267
and I guess it's for,

02:06:33.267 --> 02:06:35.160
are we doing enough in that space?

02:06:35.160 --> 02:06:38.370
Is there more that
collectively we can be doing

02:06:38.370 --> 02:06:40.080
across the NHS in terms of learning

02:06:40.080 --> 02:06:43.113
and taking action as a
result of that learning?

02:06:47.280 --> 02:06:48.930
- And Steve?
- No particular questions

02:06:48.930 --> 02:06:51.570
'cause I think Adrian, you
covered it very comprehensively,

02:06:51.570 --> 02:06:54.450
but I'm delighted you like being
in the medical directorate,

02:06:54.450 --> 02:06:57.450
but the serious point behind that was

02:06:57.450 --> 02:06:59.160
in appointing a medical director

02:06:59.160 --> 02:07:01.020
for mental health and neurodiversity,

02:07:01.020 --> 02:07:03.900
which is something we hadn't done before,

02:07:03.900 --> 02:07:05.610
we really wanted to, you know,

02:07:05.610 --> 02:07:07.860
make sure that we have the most senior

02:07:07.860 --> 02:07:09.780
clinical medical leadership

02:07:09.780 --> 02:07:10.980
supporting Claire in the programme.

02:07:10.980 --> 02:07:12.690
But also to send a real message

02:07:12.690 --> 02:07:15.510
of just how important this area is

02:07:15.510 --> 02:07:17.790
to everything that we do in NHS England.

02:07:17.790 --> 02:07:19.860
- Thank you, Vin, you have a?

02:07:19.860 --> 02:07:22.860
- Yeah, thanks, so one's
a question actually

02:07:22.860 --> 02:07:25.530
and then helpful comments hopefully.

02:07:25.530 --> 02:07:28.650
So one is in three local
authorities across England

02:07:28.650 --> 02:07:33.150
we will be piloting the
Digital NHS Health Check,

02:07:33.150 --> 02:07:38.150
which will be supported by
blood taking test kits at home.

02:07:39.250 --> 02:07:41.520
A bit like you can take
a baby's blood spot

02:07:41.520 --> 02:07:42.810
rather than currently having to go

02:07:42.810 --> 02:07:45.330
and find a health professional
to ask you the question.

02:07:45.330 --> 02:07:48.150
So if that's of value in
looking at how that might

02:07:48.150 --> 02:07:50.730
particularly help rates of uptake

02:07:50.730 --> 02:07:53.310
of the health check in
this particular population,

02:07:53.310 --> 02:07:55.620
I'd be really happy to
work with you on that.

02:07:55.620 --> 02:07:58.230
The second piece is I think
the future state vision

02:07:58.230 --> 02:08:00.630
for the app is eventually
for it to be a place

02:08:00.630 --> 02:08:04.290
where NICE-approved evidence-based apps

02:08:04.290 --> 02:08:06.945
could be recommended by practitioners.

02:08:06.945 --> 02:08:09.300
And again that's another area to look at

02:08:09.300 --> 02:08:11.580
particularly if we've got
ones coming down the pipeline,

02:08:11.580 --> 02:08:13.980
how we might start some
innovation around them.

02:08:13.980 --> 02:08:17.160
And then finally there's
some really good products

02:08:17.160 --> 02:08:19.110
being developed in the
Federated Data Platform

02:08:19.110 --> 02:08:21.120
in Cheshire and Merseyside ICB.

02:08:21.120 --> 02:08:22.350
So if you're not linked into those,

02:08:22.350 --> 02:08:23.460
I'll link you into those

02:08:23.460 --> 02:08:25.980
'cause they might have helpful ways

02:08:25.980 --> 02:08:27.930
in which we might look at
scaling those nationally

02:08:27.930 --> 02:08:29.330
if they're successful, yeah.

02:08:30.720 --> 02:08:31.553
- Thank you, Vin, so do you want to,

02:08:31.553 --> 02:08:34.290
those sort of last comments
and then we'll close that.

02:08:34.290 --> 02:08:35.280
Do you want to reflect

02:08:35.280 --> 02:08:37.340
on any of those comments or questions?

02:08:37.340 --> 02:08:41.400
- So in relation to beds and admission,

02:08:41.400 --> 02:08:42.720
nobody should be in hospital

02:08:42.720 --> 02:08:44.520
unless they really need to be in hospital.

02:08:44.520 --> 02:08:47.340
So we must always redouble our efforts

02:08:47.340 --> 02:08:50.370
to ensure that we have
good community services,

02:08:50.370 --> 02:08:52.590
we have alternatives to admission,

02:08:52.590 --> 02:08:55.860
that we can manage crises at home.

02:08:55.860 --> 02:08:59.640
So we must, and I think
the new models of care

02:08:59.640 --> 02:09:02.370
are quite exciting from
that point of view.

02:09:02.370 --> 02:09:06.120
We must again get our target
for early intervention,

02:09:06.120 --> 02:09:08.370
make sure that that's embedded.

02:09:08.370 --> 02:09:09.720
We intervene earlier,

02:09:09.720 --> 02:09:11.940
we know the outcomes are much better,

02:09:11.940 --> 02:09:15.270
so the need for admission is much reduced.

02:09:15.270 --> 02:09:19.170
We know that 10% of people
in a mental health bed

02:09:19.170 --> 02:09:20.340
don't need to be there.

02:09:20.340 --> 02:09:22.170
They're ready for discharge.

02:09:22.170 --> 02:09:23.580
That's a very complex issue.

02:09:23.580 --> 02:09:27.120
Often it is to do with
resources in the community

02:09:27.120 --> 02:09:30.540
and particularly housing
and suitable accommodations.

02:09:30.540 --> 02:09:34.860
So there are real things that
we can do to address that.

02:09:34.860 --> 02:09:38.370
And yes, we have recognised
essentially that in some areas

02:09:38.370 --> 02:09:40.440
we might need to tweak the number of beds

02:09:40.440 --> 02:09:42.090
so that we increase them.

02:09:42.090 --> 02:09:45.330
But beds is not the only
answer to all of this.

02:09:45.330 --> 02:09:48.900
And it should always be
the last, last resort.

02:09:48.900 --> 02:09:52.500
Really good to hear about
Digital Health Checks

02:09:52.500 --> 02:09:55.200
and definitely we've arranged to meet

02:09:55.200 --> 02:09:57.900
to discuss a whole variety of issues,

02:09:57.900 --> 02:10:02.460
and of course the NICE apps,
that's really good to hear.

02:10:02.460 --> 02:10:04.830
And in relation to learning,

02:10:04.830 --> 02:10:05.857
I think there is, again,

02:10:05.857 --> 02:10:08.640
there are some great models out there.

02:10:08.640 --> 02:10:11.610
We need to make sure that we
can spread that good practise

02:10:11.610 --> 02:10:13.318
and I think that central group

02:10:13.318 --> 02:10:14.643
that's going to bring together

02:10:14.643 --> 02:10:19.350
a regional team, Aidan
Fowler's team and our team

02:10:19.350 --> 02:10:22.110
I think and across both
the medical directorate

02:10:22.110 --> 02:10:24.180
but also the mental health directorate

02:10:24.180 --> 02:10:26.010
and learning disability directorate

02:10:26.010 --> 02:10:27.990
that I think there's an opportunity

02:10:27.990 --> 02:10:31.200
to really do something
much more substantial.

02:10:31.200 --> 02:10:32.130
- And just to build,

02:10:32.130 --> 02:10:33.660
especially on your first question, Chair

02:10:33.660 --> 02:10:37.380
around how we're managing
95% bed occupancy,

02:10:37.380 --> 02:10:40.020
there's a very important stat
here where length of stay

02:10:40.020 --> 02:10:43.740
has risen from 40 days to
50 days since pre-COVID.

02:10:43.740 --> 02:10:47.130
So we've effectively
lost 20% of our capacity.

02:10:47.130 --> 02:10:48.630
So we have to be managing that

02:10:48.630 --> 02:10:50.190
and we've just been running a deep dive

02:10:50.190 --> 02:10:51.540
with our colleagues in the

02:10:51.540 --> 02:10:53.460
Department of Health and Social Care

02:10:53.460 --> 02:10:55.650
precisely because we know
a lot of those solutions

02:10:55.650 --> 02:10:57.420
sit outside of the NHS.

02:10:57.420 --> 02:10:59.790
So we want to go through
that learning with them

02:10:59.790 --> 02:11:00.840
and think about how,

02:11:00.840 --> 02:11:02.970
what actions we can
take at a national level

02:11:02.970 --> 02:11:06.363
that lean not just on NHS
solutions but social care as well.

02:11:07.511 --> 02:11:08.750
- Thank you.

02:11:08.750 --> 02:11:10.440
So thank you very much
for a very good paper

02:11:10.440 --> 02:11:12.720
and a very good presentation, discussion.

02:11:12.720 --> 02:11:14.940
Really important area
and just to I think add,

02:11:14.940 --> 02:11:17.610
I mean Adrian, you said in
the introductory remarks

02:11:17.610 --> 02:11:21.120
that our thoughts are with
actually the families and friends

02:11:21.120 --> 02:11:23.190
of the three people who are,

02:11:23.190 --> 02:11:24.990
who were murdered by Valdo Calocane.

02:11:24.990 --> 02:11:27.690
And so just to say, you know,

02:11:27.690 --> 02:11:31.200
our thoughts are very much
with the, with those families.

02:11:31.200 --> 02:11:32.033
Okay,

02:11:33.180 --> 02:11:35.640
I think that brings us to two last items

02:11:35.640 --> 02:11:37.710
on the board for today.

02:11:37.710 --> 02:11:39.150
And they're both starred items

02:11:39.150 --> 02:11:40.320
which are for information only.

02:11:40.320 --> 02:11:42.660
There's a paper which
sets out the directions

02:11:42.660 --> 02:11:44.760
and mandatory requests

02:11:44.760 --> 02:11:47.160
and it's a summary of a
requirement for summary

02:11:47.160 --> 02:11:51.000
of all age continuing care directions 2024

02:11:51.000 --> 02:11:53.790
given to NHS England by
the Secretary of State.

02:11:53.790 --> 02:11:55.890
And then separately there's a quite a list

02:11:55.890 --> 02:11:58.993
of the use of the seal, mainly Julian,

02:11:58.993 --> 02:12:03.120
I think it was all Julian
actually on a range of properties.

02:12:03.120 --> 02:12:04.170
Often in primary,

02:12:04.170 --> 02:12:06.933
mainly often, actually
mainly through primary care.

02:12:08.220 --> 02:12:11.220
But we should just note those,

02:12:11.220 --> 02:12:13.680
those uses of the seal,

02:12:13.680 --> 02:12:15.830
at which point is there
any other business?

02:12:16.770 --> 02:12:19.650
And can I thank everybody
for both the private board

02:12:19.650 --> 02:12:20.880
this morning, very good board

02:12:20.880 --> 02:12:22.530
and the public board this afternoon.

02:12:22.530 --> 02:12:23.530
Thank you very much.

